1
00:01:17,894 --> 00:01:18,827
(Tensions Under the Roof)

2
00:01:19,129 --> 00:01:20,796
(The Community)

3
00:01:20,797 --> 00:01:24,033
(The Divide Has Begun)

4
00:01:24,034 --> 00:01:26,736
(The money team failed at Level One.)

5
00:01:26,737 --> 00:01:29,705
This is a reality check we didn't expect.

6
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:32,641
This is what they meant
by "a sense of reality."

7
00:01:33,343 --> 00:01:34,977
This is the Level Two question.

8
00:01:35,545 --> 00:01:39,883
A regular Army soldier enlisted
in July 2023.

9
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:43,753
If he didn't spend a single penny
and saved his entire paycheck,

10
00:01:44,187 --> 00:01:48,525
how much would he have
by the time he got discharged?

11
00:01:50,301 --> 00:01:51,862
(How much would
a regular Army soldier have...)

12
00:01:51,863 --> 00:01:53,196
(by the time he got discharged?)

13
00:01:53,197 --> 00:01:55,833
I saw that recently, but I can't remember.

14
00:01:56,233 --> 00:01:58,834
This is so frustrating.

15
00:01:58,835 --> 00:02:00,536
We're in trouble.

16
00:02:01,438 --> 00:02:04,873
We need to check how long
Army soldiers have to serve.

17
00:02:05,241 --> 00:02:08,744
Isn't it a year and eight months
these days?

18
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:10,646
Isn't it a year and a half?

19
00:02:10,647 --> 00:02:12,514
- It's 18 months.
- It's 18 months.

20
00:02:12,515 --> 00:02:13,449
A year and a half.

21
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:14,850
Then that's a year and a half.

22
00:02:14,851 --> 00:02:16,352
So we'll multiply it by 18.

23
00:02:16,353 --> 00:02:18,454
Okay. We got that one.

24
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:22,725
And their pay depends on their rank.

25
00:02:22,726 --> 00:02:26,129
People have talked a lot
about how much they make in total.

26
00:02:26,130 --> 00:02:27,697
Why can't I remember anything?

27
00:02:27,698 --> 00:02:28,931
It comes up every time
there's an election.

28
00:02:28,932 --> 00:02:29,865
I know.

29
00:02:31,035 --> 00:02:33,335
I never had to serve,
so I know nothing about the military.

30
00:02:33,336 --> 00:02:36,505
We have no idea
how long they spend at each rank.

31
00:02:36,506 --> 00:02:39,342
The length of military service
is shorter...

32
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:40,543
than when we enlisted.

33
00:02:40,544 --> 00:02:42,244
Back then, it was split into 6, 6, 8,
and 4 months.

34
00:02:42,245 --> 00:02:44,347
No. Was it 26 months in total?

35
00:02:45,315 --> 00:02:46,449
Should we use a hint?

36
00:02:46,450 --> 00:02:48,384
If we asked for a hint,

37
00:02:48,385 --> 00:02:50,686
they'd only tell us about privates.

38
00:02:50,687 --> 00:02:53,055
We'd have to just use an average.

39
00:02:53,323 --> 00:02:54,856
The new proposal said...

40
00:02:54,857 --> 00:02:57,259
They promised to raise it
to 2,000 dollars,

41
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:58,928
- but they didn't follow through.
- They failed to keep the promise.

42
00:02:58,929 --> 00:03:00,562
- Right.
- I know that much.

43
00:03:01,398 --> 00:03:04,967
They pledged to raise the soldiers' pay
to 2,000 dollars,

44
00:03:04,968 --> 00:03:07,370
which means it's lower
than 2,000 dollars now.

45
00:03:07,704 --> 00:03:10,539
As far as I remember,
it's 1,800 dollars on average.

46
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:11,774
It's 1,800 dollars on average?

47
00:03:12,509 --> 00:03:15,177
If they made 1,800 dollars a month
on average...

48
00:03:15,178 --> 00:03:17,112
and served for 18 months,

49
00:03:17,113 --> 00:03:20,716
wouldn't they be making
around 2,000 dollars by the end?

50
00:03:21,084 --> 00:03:24,219
If you were making 2,000 dollars
by the time you became a sergeant,

51
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:26,889
you'd be making more than an NCO...

52
00:03:27,023 --> 00:03:28,924
like a staff sergeant.

53
00:03:29,293 --> 00:03:31,627
Isn't that why it got controversial too?

54
00:03:31,628 --> 00:03:33,162
If they made that much,

55
00:03:33,163 --> 00:03:35,564
it could hurt the morale
of career soldiers.

56
00:03:35,565 --> 00:03:36,700
Benjamin is saying...

57
00:03:36,701 --> 00:03:39,702
the average would be lower
than 1,800 dollars.

58
00:03:40,237 --> 00:03:41,837
But that number...

59
00:03:42,505 --> 00:03:43,708
Why did the number come to mind?

60
00:03:43,709 --> 00:03:46,674
I heard that number a lot.

61
00:03:46,675 --> 00:03:48,676
- Aren't you mixing it up with 18 months?
- No.

62
00:03:48,677 --> 00:03:51,613
Like they wanted to raise it
from 1,800 dollars to 2,000 dollars.

63
00:03:51,614 --> 00:03:53,482
A soldier's average monthly pay,
1,800 dollars.

64
00:03:53,817 --> 00:03:55,084
We have to take a wild guess.

65
00:03:55,085 --> 00:03:57,386
Even if we put our heads together...

66
00:03:57,687 --> 00:04:00,055
Since that number came to our minds,

67
00:04:00,056 --> 00:04:01,056
let's base it on that.

68
00:04:01,057 --> 00:04:02,924
I just keep coming back to 1,800 dollars.

69
00:04:02,925 --> 00:04:04,226
Then that's 32,400 dollars.

70
00:04:04,251 --> 00:04:05,685
Is it 32,400 dollars?

71
00:04:05,763 --> 00:04:07,029
Whatever.

72
00:04:07,030 --> 00:04:09,298
I'm so lost. This is crazy.

73
00:04:09,299 --> 00:04:10,933
It's been a long time
since I was discharged.

74
00:04:12,002 --> 00:04:13,436
It's 32,400 dollars.

75
00:04:18,275 --> 00:04:20,209
- Incorrect.
- Seriously.

76
00:04:20,411 --> 00:04:23,046
If you try again,
500 dollars will be deducted.

77
00:04:23,047 --> 00:04:24,513
Let's just move on.

78
00:04:24,714 --> 00:04:26,348
Would you like to try again?

79
00:04:26,983 --> 00:04:29,719
- We can't use our community fund.
- We should've used a hint.

80
00:04:30,421 --> 00:04:32,288
- Okay. I got it.
- Okay.

81
00:04:33,156 --> 00:04:35,624
(Level Two Answer)

82
00:04:35,625 --> 00:04:36,425
(Army soldiers' monthly pay)

83
00:04:36,426 --> 00:04:37,259
(Answer: 14,080 dollars)

84
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:39,862
It hasn't reached that level yet.

85
00:04:39,863 --> 00:04:41,430
The campaign promise was...

86
00:04:41,631 --> 00:04:42,531
How much?

87
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:44,934
And our answer was 32,400 dollars.

88
00:04:44,935 --> 00:04:45,834
(Army soldiers' monthly pay)

89
00:04:45,859 --> 00:04:47,493
It's not even 1,000 dollars?

90
00:04:48,171 --> 00:04:49,338
I feel dumb.

91
00:04:50,173 --> 00:04:53,742
This was a major political issue
not too long ago.

92
00:04:53,743 --> 00:04:57,379
I simply thought their pay should
be raised to 2,000 dollars.

93
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:00,382
But we hadn't really looked
into the actual data...

94
00:05:00,383 --> 00:05:03,686
like how much they were making
at each rank.

95
00:05:03,687 --> 00:05:05,354
I really blamed myself.

96
00:05:05,355 --> 00:05:08,390
I felt bad that I was talking
about raising their pay...

97
00:05:08,391 --> 00:05:09,491
without even knowing the exact numbers.

98
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:11,293
I felt really bad.

99
00:05:11,294 --> 00:05:13,361
These are people who are working hard
for our country.

100
00:05:13,963 --> 00:05:16,832
Gray and other guys said 1,800 dollars.

101
00:05:16,833 --> 00:05:18,367
I knew that was wrong.

102
00:05:18,568 --> 00:05:21,370
I wanted to say it was ridiculous,

103
00:05:21,371 --> 00:05:24,273
but I didn't want to convince them
with fancy rhetoric.

104
00:05:24,274 --> 00:05:26,375
Because Benjamin isn't supposed
to be good at Korean.

105
00:05:27,377 --> 00:05:28,744
Give us the next one.

106
00:05:28,745 --> 00:05:30,046
Let's move on already.

107
00:05:30,047 --> 00:05:31,146
I'm sorry, everyone.

108
00:05:31,147 --> 00:05:32,748
This is the Level Three question.

109
00:05:33,183 --> 00:05:37,219
According to 2022 crime statistics
from the Korean National Police Agency,

110
00:05:37,521 --> 00:05:39,921
there were 24,954 victims...

111
00:05:39,922 --> 00:05:43,091
of violent crime in total.

112
00:05:43,393 --> 00:05:45,961
How many of those victims were women?

113
00:05:46,463 --> 00:05:47,663
(How many of the 24,954 victims
were women?)

114
00:05:47,664 --> 00:05:48,464
"How many?"

115
00:05:50,367 --> 00:05:51,567
This is insane.

116
00:05:51,568 --> 00:05:53,636
- That's hard.
- It's so hard.

117
00:05:58,608 --> 00:06:00,910
Violent crimes are...

118
00:06:01,245 --> 00:06:02,911
The four major violent crimes are...

119
00:06:02,912 --> 00:06:05,781
robbery, murder, rape, and arson.

120
00:06:05,782 --> 00:06:07,316
In the last statistic I saw,

121
00:06:07,317 --> 00:06:10,653
women were more than 90 percent
of sex-crime victims.

122
00:06:11,788 --> 00:06:12,855
I remember seeing that.

123
00:06:12,856 --> 00:06:16,559
I'm not sure about robbery or murder.

124
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:20,329
The male-to-female ratio is
a little different...

125
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:21,730
depending on the type of violent crime.

126
00:06:22,065 --> 00:06:24,400
In that sense, that's a lot.

127
00:06:24,635 --> 00:06:25,801
There were 24,000 victims.

128
00:06:26,185 --> 00:06:27,870
Let's see it this way.

129
00:06:27,871 --> 00:06:30,239
Let's just round that up...

130
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:33,909
to 25,000 and split it in half,

131
00:06:34,177 --> 00:06:36,278
and then we adjust the numbers from there.

132
00:06:39,116 --> 00:06:41,683
We're shooting in the dark here.
Should we use a hint?

133
00:06:41,684 --> 00:06:42,785
Let's use one.

134
00:06:42,786 --> 00:06:44,119
But...

135
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:46,288
let's not touch the community fund
for sure.

136
00:06:46,456 --> 00:06:48,791
Even if we get another chance.

137
00:06:48,792 --> 00:06:51,127
If we want to try again,

138
00:06:51,128 --> 00:06:52,362
we have to spend money that isn't ours.

139
00:06:52,363 --> 00:06:54,230
It's better to lower the amount
we take home.

140
00:06:54,231 --> 00:06:56,799
It's better to reduce
how much money we take.

141
00:06:58,501 --> 00:06:59,768
We'll use a hint.

142
00:07:00,337 --> 00:07:03,905
The prize money for this level
has been cut in half.

143
00:07:05,575 --> 00:07:09,011
(The number of female victims
between the ages of 21 and 30:7,871)

144
00:07:09,512 --> 00:07:11,046
Is it 7,871?

145
00:07:11,514 --> 00:07:13,282
Was the total 24,954?

146
00:07:13,689 --> 00:07:16,824
The female victims between 21 and 30...

147
00:07:17,054 --> 00:07:18,187
make up about 30 percent.

148
00:07:18,188 --> 00:07:19,288
(Women between 21 and 30
account for 30 percent of the victims.)

149
00:07:19,556 --> 00:07:22,190
That's so many.

150
00:07:22,659 --> 00:07:24,893
I have a feeling...

151
00:07:24,894 --> 00:07:27,796
that the reason they're asking
about female victims is...

152
00:07:27,797 --> 00:07:31,567
because they want us to think...

153
00:07:31,568 --> 00:07:33,902
about the issue of sex-crime victims.

154
00:07:33,903 --> 00:07:35,237
Then...

155
00:07:36,039 --> 00:07:38,841
why don't we guess the percentage?

156
00:07:39,008 --> 00:07:41,310
Right. You mentioned 90 percent before.

157
00:07:41,311 --> 00:07:44,947
Let's pick a percentage,
assuming it's pretty high.

158
00:07:45,915 --> 00:07:48,918
There can't be that many murders
compared to other violent crimes.

159
00:07:48,919 --> 00:07:51,086
I think we should come up with a range.

160
00:07:51,421 --> 00:07:52,722
But that's still a guess.

161
00:07:52,723 --> 00:07:55,558
- That's just an assumption too.
- Basically a guess.

162
00:07:55,559 --> 00:07:56,392
We'll be just guessing.

163
00:07:56,393 --> 00:07:59,094
Still, we've seen articles about this, so
we have some data to work with.

164
00:07:59,095 --> 00:08:01,597
Superman, just going by your gut,
what percentage would you guess?

165
00:08:01,931 --> 00:08:04,332
There must be a lot more female victims,

166
00:08:04,567 --> 00:08:06,301
so we should pick a high percentage.

167
00:08:06,302 --> 00:08:07,503
How about 70 percent?

168
00:08:07,504 --> 00:08:08,703
About 70 percent?

169
00:08:11,375 --> 00:08:13,475
Right. If it's 70 percent...

170
00:08:13,476 --> 00:08:15,344
They make us feel stupid.

171
00:08:16,613 --> 00:08:18,313
That'd be 17,500.

172
00:08:18,748 --> 00:08:19,748
If it's 70 percent?

173
00:08:19,983 --> 00:08:20,916
It's 17,500?

174
00:08:20,917 --> 00:08:22,084
Let's go.

175
00:08:22,886 --> 00:08:25,754
Hopefully, we just get lucky
and get this one right.

176
00:08:26,223 --> 00:08:27,089
Please!

177
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:28,423
It's 17,500.

178
00:08:28,424 --> 00:08:32,961
(Their guess: 17,500)

179
00:08:34,531 --> 00:08:35,966
- I'm nervous.
- Incorrect.

180
00:08:35,967 --> 00:08:37,303
That's absurd.

181
00:08:37,304 --> 00:08:40,636
If you try again,
500 dollars will be deducted.

182
00:08:43,606 --> 00:08:44,940
(Level Three Answer: 21,580)

183
00:08:44,941 --> 00:08:46,608
(Female victims: 21,580)

184
00:08:46,609 --> 00:08:48,843
That's almost all of them.

185
00:08:49,712 --> 00:08:52,714
That's over 80 percent.

186
00:08:55,051 --> 00:08:56,752
This is not good.

187
00:09:03,259 --> 00:09:04,525
I thought I was in big trouble.

188
00:09:04,526 --> 00:09:06,295
"Am I going to get impeached?"

189
00:09:06,296 --> 00:09:08,496
I stepped up, saying
I was going to make us some money.

190
00:09:08,497 --> 00:09:10,198
"What is happening?"

191
00:09:11,168 --> 00:09:12,267
We're screwed today.

192
00:09:12,636 --> 00:09:15,738
This is not the 30,000 dollars
we thought we were getting.

193
00:09:16,372 --> 00:09:17,305
"We're doomed."

194
00:09:17,906 --> 00:09:20,675
That's when my mind started to go blank.

195
00:09:28,051 --> 00:09:29,018
Gray's head...

196
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:31,687
must be spinning by now.

197
00:09:31,688 --> 00:09:33,589
So, I'm going to bring this up later.

198
00:09:33,590 --> 00:09:34,789
We should let people know.

199
00:09:34,790 --> 00:09:37,459
Let's do it. I have no filter.

200
00:09:37,460 --> 00:09:39,394
- I have to look out for myself too.
- Right.

201
00:09:39,395 --> 00:09:42,064
I'm only safe...

202
00:09:42,065 --> 00:09:44,901
if the majority understand
the situation clearly.

203
00:09:44,902 --> 00:09:45,902
Okay.

204
00:09:45,903 --> 00:09:47,203
I trust you.

205
00:09:48,705 --> 00:09:50,639
I'm really curious to see...

206
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:52,774
how this turns out today.

207
00:09:52,775 --> 00:09:54,376
Right. What are they doing?

208
00:09:54,377 --> 00:09:57,212
- What will they do?
- What they will do and how it will go.

209
00:09:57,213 --> 00:09:58,313
I know. I'm so curious.

210
00:09:58,314 --> 00:10:00,182
They all have strong personalities.

211
00:10:00,183 --> 00:10:03,152
They all have strong opinions.

212
00:10:03,153 --> 00:10:04,152
I agree.

213
00:10:04,153 --> 00:10:07,022
So it might be hard for them
to come together as a team.

214
00:10:07,023 --> 00:10:10,425
When Superman and Polar Bear
were talking earlier,

215
00:10:10,426 --> 00:10:12,494
- they were so different.
- I know!

216
00:10:12,495 --> 00:10:13,662
They're the opposite.

217
00:10:13,663 --> 00:10:15,497
There should be only one head.

218
00:10:16,199 --> 00:10:18,733
But that team has two heads.

219
00:10:19,102 --> 00:10:21,070
Superman and Polar Bear.

220
00:10:21,071 --> 00:10:22,671
It's possible that they fail today.

221
00:10:28,153 --> 00:10:33,190
- Ted and Gray are intense too,
- Gray.

222
00:10:33,215 --> 00:10:35,550
but they keep it under wraps.

223
00:10:35,551 --> 00:10:37,785
Gray is especially like that.

224
00:10:37,810 --> 00:10:40,078
Gray is good at politics.

225
00:10:40,290 --> 00:10:42,992
He gives off the vibe of a strategist.

226
00:10:43,693 --> 00:10:44,960
So actually...

227
00:10:47,397 --> 00:10:49,231
Let me tell you what happened yesterday.

228
00:10:51,367 --> 00:10:54,402
I'd say we should keep this
within the group.

229
00:10:54,403 --> 00:10:57,038
Dark Knight and I weren't sure
if we should share this,

230
00:10:57,039 --> 00:10:59,107
- but we decided to do it.
- I said I didn't care.

231
00:10:59,108 --> 00:11:00,008
Right.

232
00:11:00,276 --> 00:11:03,411
One of the things Gray said to me was...

233
00:11:03,412 --> 00:11:05,881
that we should form an alliance.

234
00:11:05,882 --> 00:11:08,283
- So we can survive until the end.
- Right.

235
00:11:08,284 --> 00:11:10,685
- Let's make this work.
- Okay.

236
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:13,121
Did Gray say that first?

237
00:11:13,122 --> 00:11:14,055
Yes.

238
00:11:14,190 --> 00:11:16,858
I was going to stay loyal...

239
00:11:16,859 --> 00:11:18,094
and do my best.

240
00:11:18,095 --> 00:11:19,861
The people who smoke often are...

241
00:11:19,862 --> 00:11:22,764
me, Gray, and Dark Knight.

242
00:11:22,765 --> 00:11:24,366
When we went out for a smoke,

243
00:11:24,367 --> 00:11:27,202
I asked Dark Knight
if he wanted to join us,

244
00:11:27,203 --> 00:11:29,404
and I reached out...

245
00:11:29,405 --> 00:11:30,672
to shake his hand.

246
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:34,443
Then he said he voted for me.

247
00:11:34,444 --> 00:11:37,078
Gray had said...

248
00:11:37,648 --> 00:11:39,215
he voted for me too.

249
00:11:39,216 --> 00:11:40,916
But I only received one vote.

250
00:11:40,917 --> 00:11:43,686
That's when I got super confused.

251
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:46,121
It means one of them lied to me...

252
00:11:46,122 --> 00:11:48,457
on the first day.

253
00:11:48,458 --> 00:11:51,259
Spies usually make their move
right from the start.

254
00:11:53,229 --> 00:11:55,564
Nothing was really going on
on the first day,

255
00:11:55,565 --> 00:11:58,099
and he wanted to form an alliance.

256
00:11:58,100 --> 00:11:59,968
He was stirring up trouble.

257
00:11:59,969 --> 00:12:01,136
Exactly.

258
00:12:01,137 --> 00:12:02,904
He was splitting the team apart.

259
00:12:02,905 --> 00:12:04,739
If he wasn't the spy,
would he really have done that?

260
00:12:05,741 --> 00:12:07,043
I'm so convinced.

261
00:12:07,044 --> 00:12:09,912
Now that we're aware of what's going on,

262
00:12:09,913 --> 00:12:12,948
we should keep an eye on him.

263
00:12:13,549 --> 00:12:16,284
We need to keep a close eye on the spy,

264
00:12:16,285 --> 00:12:19,354
but we can't let him know we're onto him.

265
00:12:20,323 --> 00:12:22,357
If he catches on,

266
00:12:22,358 --> 00:12:24,160
he'll definitely start looking...

267
00:12:24,161 --> 00:12:25,994
for a way around it.

268
00:12:25,995 --> 00:12:27,764
- I thought the opposite.
- Is that so?

269
00:12:27,765 --> 00:12:29,332
- I thought...
- Openly?

270
00:12:29,333 --> 00:12:31,066
we should bring this up...

271
00:12:31,067 --> 00:12:33,368
when everyone's together.

272
00:12:33,369 --> 00:12:36,238
If there's only one spy,

273
00:12:36,239 --> 00:12:37,773
it's 11 against 1.

274
00:12:37,774 --> 00:12:40,709
That would put a lot of pressure on them.

275
00:12:41,144 --> 00:12:45,181
We'd have everyone sitting around...

276
00:12:45,182 --> 00:12:46,949
who could fact-check things right away.

277
00:12:46,950 --> 00:12:50,219
That would be the best way
to figure out if he's the spy.

278
00:12:52,188 --> 00:12:54,722
- When everyone's together.
- When we're all together.

279
00:12:55,558 --> 00:12:57,158
I could tell everyone everything...

280
00:12:57,159 --> 00:12:58,360
if I wanted to,

281
00:12:58,361 --> 00:13:00,695
but if he's actually the spy,

282
00:13:00,696 --> 00:13:02,597
I can't guarantee my own safety.

283
00:13:02,598 --> 00:13:05,101
If you expose him...

284
00:13:05,102 --> 00:13:07,168
and he immediately goes after you,

285
00:13:07,303 --> 00:13:09,436
that would make it obvious
that Gray is the spy.

286
00:13:09,437 --> 00:13:11,906
But I don't want to be eliminated early.

287
00:13:12,008 --> 00:13:13,875
I don't want to sacrifice myself...

288
00:13:14,077 --> 00:13:16,177
just to expose the spy.

289
00:13:16,178 --> 00:13:18,780
No one wants to sacrifice themselves,
actually.

290
00:13:18,781 --> 00:13:20,849
- He's honest.
- I'm being honest.

291
00:13:23,986 --> 00:13:26,321
I knew something might happen.

292
00:13:26,322 --> 00:13:27,689
We were split into groups of six.

293
00:13:27,690 --> 00:13:30,124
It was the perfect way to plant
the seeds of division.

294
00:13:31,127 --> 00:13:34,830
People think they're defending themselves.

295
00:13:34,831 --> 00:13:36,898
Because they feel that way,

296
00:13:36,899 --> 00:13:40,335
they try to get people on their side
and keep secrets within the group.

297
00:13:40,336 --> 00:13:43,305
That's how they put up boundaries.

298
00:13:43,306 --> 00:13:46,041
I saw that as a sign...

299
00:13:46,042 --> 00:13:48,509
that we could start falling apart...

300
00:13:48,510 --> 00:13:50,445
and that our unity could start to weaken.

301
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:54,283
Michael takes a lot of the initiative,

302
00:13:54,284 --> 00:13:55,817
and he's always talking.

303
00:13:55,986 --> 00:13:57,252
I've been focused...

304
00:13:57,253 --> 00:14:00,188
on finding the spy
since the very first day as well.

305
00:14:00,189 --> 00:14:01,823
I'll let him do his thing,

306
00:14:01,824 --> 00:14:02,958
even if he fails.

307
00:14:02,959 --> 00:14:05,761
If there's anything I can do to help,
I will.

308
00:14:08,297 --> 00:14:10,331
Are we going to tell everyone...

309
00:14:10,332 --> 00:14:11,933
what we talked about?

310
00:14:12,635 --> 00:14:15,003
Well, it's up to you.

311
00:14:15,004 --> 00:14:17,238
If you think it'll put you in danger,
you don't have to.

312
00:14:17,239 --> 00:14:19,674
If I expose the spy,

313
00:14:19,675 --> 00:14:21,109
I want to ask everyone here...

314
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:23,344
to have my back.

315
00:14:23,546 --> 00:14:26,148
The only way we can eliminate the spy is
by correctly identifying them.

316
00:14:26,149 --> 00:14:27,782
If that's the case,

317
00:14:28,084 --> 00:14:29,852
there's actually no way...

318
00:14:29,853 --> 00:14:31,787
we can't stop them from identifying them.

319
00:14:31,788 --> 00:14:34,088
So there are two ways.

320
00:14:34,089 --> 00:14:36,992
I could become the leader tomorrow...

321
00:14:38,494 --> 00:14:40,629
and get an immunity card.

322
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:44,499
So do you want to be the leader tomorrow?

323
00:14:44,500 --> 00:14:45,400
Yes.

324
00:14:46,302 --> 00:14:48,136
Then let's talk after doing the dishes.

325
00:14:48,137 --> 00:14:48,937
Okay.

326
00:14:48,938 --> 00:14:50,839
- Tell everyone about that.
- Okay.

327
00:14:51,713 --> 00:14:52,613
Sounds good.

328
00:14:53,543 --> 00:14:55,544
It's about the spy.

329
00:14:56,345 --> 00:14:59,414
Let's bring it up when they come back.

330
00:14:59,415 --> 00:15:01,215
Instead,

331
00:15:01,651 --> 00:15:04,452
I need you to make me the leader tomorrow.

332
00:15:04,453 --> 00:15:05,520
Doesn't it sound good?

333
00:15:06,422 --> 00:15:07,922
You're worried about it.

334
00:15:08,191 --> 00:15:11,359
If we give you our six votes,
you'll become the leader.

335
00:15:11,360 --> 00:15:13,061
Then you'll get an immunity card too.

336
00:15:13,062 --> 00:15:15,029
Which means we completely trust him.

337
00:15:15,030 --> 00:15:16,398
I'm not sure about that.

338
00:15:16,399 --> 00:15:17,899
He wants to be the leader.

339
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:19,634
If Michael was the spy...

340
00:15:19,635 --> 00:15:21,469
and we promised him that,

341
00:15:21,470 --> 00:15:23,471
he could attack someone tonight...

342
00:15:23,472 --> 00:15:24,839
and get an immunity card tomorrow.

343
00:15:26,375 --> 00:15:27,709
- If I were the spy?
- Yes.

344
00:15:28,844 --> 00:15:29,977
I'm innocent.

345
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:32,113
Should I make this gesture?

346
00:15:32,782 --> 00:15:34,116
I could do this.

347
00:15:34,418 --> 00:15:36,151
- What's with that?
- Even when I'm a good son.

348
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:38,987
I have a suggestion.

349
00:15:38,988 --> 00:15:42,424
When they get back,
let's tell them as soon as possible.

350
00:15:42,425 --> 00:15:43,291
As soon as possible.

351
00:15:43,726 --> 00:15:46,695
If Gray was actually the spy,

352
00:15:46,963 --> 00:15:48,997
I'd be his number-one target.

353
00:15:49,332 --> 00:15:50,565
Even if he's not,

354
00:15:50,767 --> 00:15:53,134
it would completely destroy
the trust between us.

355
00:15:53,135 --> 00:15:54,669
Right. You'll become enemies.

356
00:15:54,670 --> 00:15:56,438
I'm in a different position
from all of you,

357
00:15:56,439 --> 00:15:58,006
so I feel a little desperate.

358
00:15:58,007 --> 00:15:59,941
I think...

359
00:15:59,942 --> 00:16:02,210
I'm easy to figure out.

360
00:16:02,211 --> 00:16:03,144
Since you have a high score.

361
00:16:03,145 --> 00:16:05,013
I could be eliminated tonight too.

362
00:16:05,014 --> 00:16:06,815
Making it through tonight is...

363
00:16:06,816 --> 00:16:08,083
a big risk for me.

364
00:16:08,084 --> 00:16:09,684
- You're desperate.
- Yes.

365
00:16:10,019 --> 00:16:12,153
If Michael is ever in danger...

366
00:16:12,154 --> 00:16:14,689
of getting eliminated,

367
00:16:15,024 --> 00:16:17,359
we could promise him...

368
00:16:17,627 --> 00:16:21,162
that whoever has immunity will save him.

369
00:16:21,163 --> 00:16:23,765
But if he becomes the leader,

370
00:16:24,133 --> 00:16:26,034
he'll get an immunity card anyway.

371
00:16:28,138 --> 00:16:30,505
She made it sound like...

372
00:16:30,506 --> 00:16:34,709
I should use my immunity card
so Michael can attack Gray.

373
00:16:34,710 --> 00:16:36,711
Whether he openly went after Gray or not,

374
00:16:36,712 --> 00:16:39,181
it didn't make any difference to me.

375
00:16:39,182 --> 00:16:41,383
Hippo was the one who suggested it.

376
00:16:41,384 --> 00:16:44,619
Why would I use my immunity card
because you're desperate?

377
00:16:45,087 --> 00:16:47,088
I didn't like...

378
00:16:47,089 --> 00:16:48,589
that she proposed that.

379
00:16:49,492 --> 00:16:50,959
I'll go have a smoke.

380
00:16:51,160 --> 00:16:53,261
Michael, you can stay.
If we went together,

381
00:16:53,629 --> 00:16:54,929
we'd look suspicious.

382
00:16:55,264 --> 00:16:57,165
Let's smoke one at a time.

383
00:16:57,933 --> 00:16:59,367
Come on.

384
00:16:59,802 --> 00:17:00,702
I'll be back.

385
00:17:02,471 --> 00:17:04,405
We'll go watch them.

386
00:17:04,406 --> 00:17:05,406
Okay.

387
00:17:07,843 --> 00:17:08,876
Me too.

388
00:17:08,877 --> 00:17:11,879
Come with us. I'll give you a cigarette.

389
00:17:12,582 --> 00:17:14,649
Wait. I'm so confused.

390
00:17:17,821 --> 00:17:18,687
Do you smoke?

391
00:17:18,688 --> 00:17:20,489
Yes. Thank you.

392
00:17:20,823 --> 00:17:24,092
I'm suddenly feeling anxious.

393
00:17:24,093 --> 00:17:25,760
I hate it when things get messy.

394
00:17:26,796 --> 00:17:28,563
They talked about it during a smoke break.

395
00:17:28,831 --> 00:17:31,466
I wanted to join their smoke break...

396
00:17:31,467 --> 00:17:33,835
and see what they talk about.

397
00:17:37,406 --> 00:17:38,373
Honestly...

398
00:17:39,408 --> 00:17:40,241
It's nothing.

399
00:17:40,242 --> 00:17:41,309
- What?
- What?

400
00:17:44,580 --> 00:17:45,847
"There's one spy."

401
00:17:46,415 --> 00:17:48,249
Isn't this just stirring up trouble?

402
00:17:48,517 --> 00:17:50,051
It's creating a ton of conflict.

403
00:17:50,886 --> 00:17:52,220
I even had this thought.

404
00:17:52,221 --> 00:17:54,889
If he was the spy, maybe he's trying...

405
00:17:54,890 --> 00:17:56,424
to split the two of them up,

406
00:17:56,425 --> 00:17:58,960
- I thought about that too.
- even after getting two hearts.

407
00:17:58,961 --> 00:18:01,028
No one can fact-check that.

408
00:18:02,238 --> 00:18:04,505
Right. Let's think carefully about this.

409
00:18:04,867 --> 00:18:05,934
I just stayed quiet.

410
00:18:07,203 --> 00:18:09,037
I had a lot on my mind, listening to him.

411
00:18:09,309 --> 00:18:10,372
I know.

412
00:18:13,943 --> 00:18:15,043
They couldn't hear us, right?

413
00:18:23,586 --> 00:18:27,255
He was trying to create more cracks
little by little.

414
00:18:27,590 --> 00:18:30,258
But things are going...

415
00:18:30,659 --> 00:18:32,727
in a totally different direction
than I expected.

416
00:18:32,728 --> 00:18:35,496
Including me, Sugar, Genie,

417
00:18:35,497 --> 00:18:37,098
and Hippo, who I made eye contact with,

418
00:18:37,099 --> 00:18:38,867
we all started suspecting Michael more...

419
00:18:38,868 --> 00:18:41,435
after he said that.

420
00:18:41,436 --> 00:18:43,372
We all found it suspicious...

421
00:18:43,373 --> 00:18:45,139
that the spy had been exposed so easily.

422
00:18:45,541 --> 00:18:48,310
If I expose this,

423
00:18:48,311 --> 00:18:50,212
it's obviously going to put me at risk.

424
00:18:50,213 --> 00:18:51,913
So in return,

425
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:54,141
I want an immunity card...

426
00:18:54,450 --> 00:18:56,484
for doing that.

427
00:18:56,753 --> 00:18:58,785
- That's what I'm saying.
- So...

428
00:18:58,786 --> 00:18:59,955
you want to get all six of us on board...

429
00:18:59,956 --> 00:19:02,089
before the other team gets back.

430
00:19:02,558 --> 00:19:05,627
- I'm not sure. I'm sorry.
- I don't know.

431
00:19:05,628 --> 00:19:06,561
They're speaking their minds.

432
00:19:06,562 --> 00:19:08,596
We were just sitting here,

433
00:19:08,597 --> 00:19:11,032
and you came over and started telling us.

434
00:19:11,233 --> 00:19:15,069
It's not like we wanted that information.

435
00:19:15,070 --> 00:19:17,772
Thanks for sharing, but...

436
00:19:17,773 --> 00:19:20,841
Then just don't tell them
what happened here...

437
00:19:20,842 --> 00:19:21,810
when they get back.

438
00:19:21,811 --> 00:19:23,377
You don't want us to tell them...

439
00:19:23,378 --> 00:19:26,480
about what we talked about here.

440
00:19:26,481 --> 00:19:27,715
- Exactly.
- Is that it?

441
00:19:31,821 --> 00:19:32,721
It's raining.

442
00:19:33,957 --> 00:19:36,691
- It started raining.
- It's raining, and there's thunder.

443
00:19:37,193 --> 00:19:38,125
(Current earnings: zero dollars)

444
00:19:38,150 --> 00:19:39,684
This is the Level Four question.

445
00:19:40,129 --> 00:19:41,662
According to the 2022 census,

446
00:19:41,965 --> 00:19:44,265
what was the number...

447
00:19:44,434 --> 00:19:46,501
of one-person households nationwide?

448
00:19:46,502 --> 00:19:47,936
Gosh!

449
00:19:47,937 --> 00:19:49,737
- I've seen that.
- I saw that too.

450
00:19:49,738 --> 00:19:52,006
I saw the number in an article.

451
00:19:52,341 --> 00:19:55,043
What's the point of reading an article?
We won't remember it.

452
00:19:55,044 --> 00:19:58,413
If you take away the internet,
we know nothing.

453
00:19:58,716 --> 00:20:00,148
Wait. Then...

454
00:20:00,149 --> 00:20:03,853
I'm pretty sure it was over a third.

455
00:20:03,854 --> 00:20:06,988
Me too.
I heard it was close to 40 percent.

456
00:20:06,989 --> 00:20:08,657
It's between 30 and 40 percent.

457
00:20:09,324 --> 00:20:10,559
Do we know the total population...

458
00:20:10,560 --> 00:20:12,060
from the census?

459
00:20:12,061 --> 00:20:13,795
- We don't.
- We have to figure that out.

460
00:20:14,496 --> 00:20:17,131
Isn't the population around 50 million?

461
00:20:17,266 --> 00:20:20,101
But that's the number of households,
not people.

462
00:20:20,418 --> 00:20:22,003
Right. It's not the number of people.

463
00:20:22,004 --> 00:20:23,805
- Households.
- This is driving me crazy.

464
00:20:23,806 --> 00:20:25,306
- I'm losing my mind.
- We can do it.

465
00:20:25,307 --> 00:20:26,641
It must be somewhere in our brains.

466
00:20:27,277 --> 00:20:30,312
Let's think about it.
One-person households.

467
00:20:30,546 --> 00:20:32,213
I don't know the number of households.

468
00:20:34,950 --> 00:20:37,252
Should we use the hint and make a guess,

469
00:20:37,253 --> 00:20:40,421
or should we risk being wrong
and spend 500 dollars?

470
00:20:40,422 --> 00:20:42,557
We don't have money now, right?

471
00:20:42,858 --> 00:20:44,725
We only have 1,900 dollars.

472
00:20:44,726 --> 00:20:47,195
Once we start using the community fund,

473
00:20:47,196 --> 00:20:48,663
we're basically gambling.

474
00:20:48,664 --> 00:20:50,665
If we spend the community fund...

475
00:20:50,666 --> 00:20:52,834
just because we want to be heroes,

476
00:20:52,835 --> 00:20:55,103
- we could all end up screwed.
- Right. No.

477
00:20:55,104 --> 00:20:58,439
Then should we see the hint?

478
00:20:58,742 --> 00:21:00,676
That'd be better than just guessing.

479
00:21:00,677 --> 00:21:01,610
(Using a hint)

480
00:21:01,611 --> 00:21:03,244
- Give us a hint.
- Give us a hint.

481
00:21:03,245 --> 00:21:06,614
The prize money for this level
has been cut in half.

482
00:21:06,615 --> 00:21:07,649
Please.

483
00:21:07,883 --> 00:21:11,219
(The total number of households in 2022:
22,383,187)

484
00:21:11,220 --> 00:21:12,486
- What?
- What?

485
00:21:12,487 --> 00:21:14,355
- We can work with that.
- All right!

486
00:21:15,157 --> 00:21:17,125
It's 22,383,187.

487
00:21:17,126 --> 00:21:18,326
That was worth it.

488
00:21:18,327 --> 00:21:19,293
This is a great hint.

489
00:21:19,628 --> 00:21:23,064
I remember it was
between 30 and 35 percent.

490
00:21:23,299 --> 00:21:24,466
Between 30 and 35 percent?

491
00:21:25,901 --> 00:21:27,235
It's a plus or minus five percent, right?

492
00:21:27,236 --> 00:21:28,903
Plus or minus.

493
00:21:28,904 --> 00:21:32,073
So if we pick a percentage,

494
00:21:32,408 --> 00:21:34,609
we can multiply it by five percent.

495
00:21:35,011 --> 00:21:36,411
Let me show you.

496
00:21:38,614 --> 00:21:42,050
Let's say we picked 32 percent.

497
00:21:42,051 --> 00:21:43,985
If we multiply this
by the total number of households,

498
00:21:43,986 --> 00:21:45,220
we'll get the answer.

499
00:21:45,221 --> 00:21:49,524
We'll go five percent above
and below this.

500
00:21:49,725 --> 00:21:52,627
If we multiply this by five percent,
that's 1.6 percent.

501
00:21:52,628 --> 00:21:56,664
So that's plus or minus 1.6 percent.

502
00:21:56,966 --> 00:21:58,501
That's so little.

503
00:21:58,502 --> 00:22:00,636
It covers 30.4 percent...

504
00:22:00,637 --> 00:22:03,372
- To 33.6 percent.
- to 33.6 percent.

505
00:22:03,373 --> 00:22:04,439
Like this.

506
00:22:04,774 --> 00:22:05,840
Only that much?

507
00:22:06,208 --> 00:22:07,942
So that's one range.

508
00:22:08,037 --> 00:22:10,438
Can we try to cover up to ten percent?

509
00:22:10,580 --> 00:22:11,980
We can just move the range up.

510
00:22:11,981 --> 00:22:16,417
If we move it up,
we can add another three percent here.

511
00:22:16,418 --> 00:22:18,420
What do you think? Should we do that?

512
00:22:18,421 --> 00:22:20,188
- That number?
- Thirty-two percent.

513
00:22:20,255 --> 00:22:21,255
All right.

514
00:22:21,256 --> 00:22:23,257
Let's try that.

515
00:22:23,392 --> 00:22:25,960
That's 7,162,619.84.

516
00:22:25,961 --> 00:22:27,295
Two decimal places.

517
00:22:27,296 --> 00:22:28,395
Let's go with 7,162,620.

518
00:22:28,396 --> 00:22:30,732
That's correct.

519
00:22:31,066 --> 00:22:33,200
Is it 7,162,620?

520
00:22:34,603 --> 00:22:35,536
Let's go.

521
00:22:35,805 --> 00:22:37,238
- Please!
- Please!

522
00:22:37,272 --> 00:22:38,405
- Please!
- Please!

523
00:22:38,406 --> 00:22:39,739
I'm tired of guessing it wrong.

524
00:22:39,740 --> 00:22:41,442
We want the right answer!

525
00:22:42,778 --> 00:22:46,848
It's 7,162,620.

526
00:22:46,849 --> 00:22:48,449
(Their estimate of the number
of one-person households)

527
00:22:52,554 --> 00:22:53,554
Correct.

528
00:22:54,590 --> 00:22:55,523
Correct.

529
00:22:56,592 --> 00:22:58,526
(Level 4 Prize: 4,000 dollars earned)

530
00:23:03,432 --> 00:23:04,432
Benjamin!

531
00:23:05,367 --> 00:23:06,367
One-third!

532
00:23:07,102 --> 00:23:08,202
This is...

533
00:23:08,804 --> 00:23:10,138
One in three households!

534
00:23:13,642 --> 00:23:14,675
My gosh.

535
00:23:14,676 --> 00:23:15,676
Benjamin!

536
00:23:16,111 --> 00:23:17,745
It feels like a dream.

537
00:23:17,980 --> 00:23:20,715
- You played a big role.
- Your approach was...

538
00:23:21,150 --> 00:23:22,050
I'm flattered.

539
00:23:22,151 --> 00:23:24,018
We made money
without touching the community fund.

540
00:23:24,721 --> 00:23:26,054
I got goosebumps.

541
00:23:26,456 --> 00:23:28,723
- We almost got it wrong.
- We barely made it.

542
00:23:28,724 --> 00:23:29,791
We almost failed.

543
00:23:30,026 --> 00:23:32,694
That's true! We almost got it wrong.

544
00:23:32,695 --> 00:23:34,695
I'm glad we brought Benjamin.

545
00:23:35,564 --> 00:23:37,498
As everyone knows,

546
00:23:37,499 --> 00:23:40,635
Benjamin is a math genius.
He did all the math for us.

547
00:23:40,636 --> 00:23:42,971
Benjamin was a huge help.

548
00:23:42,972 --> 00:23:46,174
He was the only person...

549
00:23:46,175 --> 00:23:47,843
who could do the math by hand.

550
00:23:50,312 --> 00:23:51,445
I wasn't that happy.

551
00:23:51,446 --> 00:23:52,947
(Laughing)

552
00:23:52,948 --> 00:23:55,316
It was so easy to act like that.

553
00:23:55,317 --> 00:23:56,417
I'm even pretending I lived abroad.

554
00:23:56,718 --> 00:23:58,886
I have four points. I'm the weakest.

555
00:23:59,088 --> 00:24:00,555
I'm even a spy.

556
00:24:00,556 --> 00:24:02,756
I'm digging myself into a hole
with all these lies.

557
00:24:02,757 --> 00:24:05,427
I'm doing the best I can with what I have.

558
00:24:05,428 --> 00:24:06,560
I'm pretending to work hard.

559
00:24:06,561 --> 00:24:10,064
I just did some math,
and they were impressed.

560
00:24:10,065 --> 00:24:12,633
I didn't even have to lift a finger.

561
00:24:12,634 --> 00:24:14,468
I wrote down the calculations,

562
00:24:14,469 --> 00:24:15,871
and they were so relieved.

563
00:24:15,872 --> 00:24:18,639
I didn't understand
what they were so relieved about.

564
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:20,307
I just did some basic multiplication.

565
00:24:20,441 --> 00:24:22,876
They're definitely not math people.

566
00:24:23,046 --> 00:24:23,978
They're really not.

567
00:24:24,179 --> 00:24:25,646
They're good people.

568
00:24:25,647 --> 00:24:27,882
I hope they stay close with me
and get along...

569
00:24:28,117 --> 00:24:29,084
for a long time.

570
00:24:30,286 --> 00:24:31,652
Even if we lowball it,

571
00:24:31,653 --> 00:24:33,153
we can at least cover food
through tomorrow.

572
00:24:33,154 --> 00:24:34,821
- We didn't spend the community fund.
- No, we didn't.

573
00:24:34,822 --> 00:24:36,190
Let's try the last question.

574
00:24:36,191 --> 00:24:38,292
All right. Level Five!

575
00:24:38,594 --> 00:24:40,995
As of September 2022,

576
00:24:40,996 --> 00:24:46,968
Statistics Korea had 51,292
convenience stores registered nationwide.

577
00:24:47,170 --> 00:24:51,940
Then, how many convenience stores
would there be in Seoul?

578
00:24:52,808 --> 00:24:53,741
How many convenience stores?

579
00:24:53,742 --> 00:24:56,444
(How many convenience stores
would there be in Seoul?)

580
00:24:56,445 --> 00:24:57,779
The number of convenience stores in Seoul?

581
00:24:59,414 --> 00:25:00,547
I have no idea.

582
00:25:00,548 --> 00:25:03,017
Wait. How about this?

583
00:25:03,018 --> 00:25:03,884
All right.

584
00:25:04,553 --> 00:25:07,488
There are 50 million people in Korea.

585
00:25:07,855 --> 00:25:09,623
If there are
roughly 50,000 convenience stores,

586
00:25:09,624 --> 00:25:12,926
that's 1 convenience store
for every 1,000 people.

587
00:25:12,927 --> 00:25:14,228
Population density!

588
00:25:14,229 --> 00:25:19,366
Let's say 1 convenience store covers
1,000 people.

589
00:25:19,701 --> 00:25:22,703
Seoul has around 10
to 11 million people, right?

590
00:25:22,704 --> 00:25:25,740
A lot of people commute
in and out of Seoul,

591
00:25:25,741 --> 00:25:28,208
so it'd be around ten million.

592
00:25:28,377 --> 00:25:30,178
Then arithmetically,

593
00:25:30,379 --> 00:25:32,347
one-fifth of the country's population is
in Seoul.

594
00:25:32,681 --> 00:25:34,682
So you could estimate...

595
00:25:34,683 --> 00:25:38,119
around 10,000 convenience stores...

596
00:25:38,120 --> 00:25:40,354
in Seoul.

597
00:25:41,223 --> 00:25:42,723
That's the arithmetic answer.

598
00:25:42,724 --> 00:25:44,559
But in real life,

599
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:47,963
convenience stores are likely
to be more concentrated in Seoul.

600
00:25:47,964 --> 00:25:50,265
- I'm saying 10,000 is the lowest number.
- Right.

601
00:25:50,266 --> 00:25:52,233
- The minimum has to be 10,000.
- I agree.

602
00:25:52,234 --> 00:25:54,268
We have to account for foot traffic.

603
00:25:54,269 --> 00:25:56,437
Places like Gangnam and Hongdae...

604
00:25:56,438 --> 00:25:59,572
have way more people moving around
and living there.

605
00:25:59,573 --> 00:26:02,176
If 10,000 is the minimum,

606
00:26:02,177 --> 00:26:04,178
what range can we cover?

607
00:26:04,179 --> 00:26:05,813
Let me do the math.

608
00:26:06,415 --> 00:26:07,415
Well...

609
00:26:11,119 --> 00:26:16,490
(If the answer is 11,000,
it covers a range from 10,450 to 11,550.)

610
00:26:16,491 --> 00:26:23,631
(Benjamin's margins of error)

611
00:26:23,632 --> 00:26:26,667
All we need to do is agree
on which of the five ranges...

612
00:26:26,668 --> 00:26:27,768
to go with.

613
00:26:28,002 --> 00:26:29,536
I want to choose the middle one.

614
00:26:29,671 --> 00:26:30,771
The one in the middle.

615
00:26:30,906 --> 00:26:32,573
- You mean 13,400?
- Yes.

616
00:26:32,874 --> 00:26:34,942
I think it's either the 3rd
or the 4th one.

617
00:26:34,943 --> 00:26:37,511
I personally want to choose
the second one.

618
00:26:37,913 --> 00:26:39,247
But...

619
00:26:39,248 --> 00:26:42,183
the third range can cover the 12,000s.

620
00:26:43,019 --> 00:26:46,687
I think this is a reasonable range.

621
00:26:47,356 --> 00:26:48,991
What if we try 13,000?

622
00:26:48,992 --> 00:26:52,060
Sounds good.
We're not going to make a wild guess.

623
00:26:52,061 --> 00:26:53,394
We're going to see the hint.

624
00:26:56,964 --> 00:26:58,898
I'm worried about something else now.

625
00:26:59,835 --> 00:27:00,801
What is it?

626
00:27:00,802 --> 00:27:03,404
If we get a useful hint like we did...

627
00:27:03,405 --> 00:27:04,739
in Level Four,

628
00:27:05,732 --> 00:27:06,807
it's worth taking.

629
00:27:08,211 --> 00:27:10,544
If we use a hint, our prize money...

630
00:27:11,513 --> 00:27:12,546
will be 8,000 dollars.

631
00:27:12,547 --> 00:27:14,181
I want to try again at least once.

632
00:27:14,182 --> 00:27:16,450
I also think we should go all in on this.

633
00:27:16,752 --> 00:27:18,086
We didn't use a lot of hints.

634
00:27:18,087 --> 00:27:19,019
If we're confident,

635
00:27:19,020 --> 00:27:20,922
it's definitely better not to use a hint.

636
00:27:20,923 --> 00:27:24,091
If we try three times
and get it right on our fourth try,

637
00:27:24,092 --> 00:27:26,694
we'll walk away with 20,000 dollars,
including what we've already won.

638
00:27:27,029 --> 00:27:29,497
- I say we just go for it.
- I agree.

639
00:27:30,899 --> 00:27:32,766
I think if we use a hint,
we can get it right.

640
00:27:33,402 --> 00:27:35,335
Keep trying without a hint?

641
00:27:35,336 --> 00:27:36,504
I'm not sure about that.

642
00:27:36,505 --> 00:27:37,672
Let's try just once, then.

643
00:27:38,040 --> 00:27:40,775
I don't think it's the top
or bottom range.

644
00:27:40,776 --> 00:27:43,411
If it's 1 of the 3, that's 33.3 percent.

645
00:27:44,213 --> 00:27:45,713
That's a pretty good chance.

646
00:27:45,714 --> 00:27:46,580
It's 33.3 percent.

647
00:27:46,948 --> 00:27:48,749
But at the same time,

648
00:27:48,750 --> 00:27:51,652
we came up
with those ranges somewhat arbitrarily.

649
00:27:51,653 --> 00:27:54,187
We can't be sure the correct answer is
in those ranges.

650
00:27:54,188 --> 00:27:58,025
So, the real question is,
are we actually confident enough...

651
00:27:58,026 --> 00:28:00,294
to try once with that number?

652
00:28:00,295 --> 00:28:02,229
I think we're pretty confident.

653
00:28:02,230 --> 00:28:04,498
Aren't we just going with our gut?

654
00:28:04,499 --> 00:28:05,532
A number that feels familiar.

655
00:28:05,533 --> 00:28:06,601
A number that sounds about right.

656
00:28:06,602 --> 00:28:08,503
That's not how I see it.

657
00:28:08,504 --> 00:28:10,571
They're private businesses,
not public institutions.

658
00:28:10,572 --> 00:28:12,539
They probably calculated
the numbers carefully.

659
00:28:12,540 --> 00:28:15,242
It makes sense to start with 10,000
and adjust from there.

660
00:28:15,243 --> 00:28:16,710
And I don't think it'd be...

661
00:28:16,711 --> 00:28:19,646
too far above the baseline
of 1 store for every 1,000 people.

662
00:28:19,671 --> 00:28:22,073
I don't think it would go
all the way up to 15,000.

663
00:28:22,818 --> 00:28:26,987
This activity wasn't just
about making money.

664
00:28:27,222 --> 00:28:29,690
It was also a test...

665
00:28:29,691 --> 00:28:31,992
of how we handle the community fund.

666
00:28:31,993 --> 00:28:34,228
We didn't realize that until Level Five.

667
00:28:34,363 --> 00:28:37,131
Can we spend the community fund
without their permission?

668
00:28:37,132 --> 00:28:38,933
I thought it would be better
to protect the money...

669
00:28:38,934 --> 00:28:41,836
and at least come back with something.

670
00:28:41,837 --> 00:28:44,472
But at the same time,
if we always play it this safe,

671
00:28:45,073 --> 00:28:46,573
when are we ever going
to become more prosperous?

672
00:28:47,309 --> 00:28:48,142
This is why...

673
00:28:49,445 --> 00:28:50,945
politicians fight all the time.

674
00:28:51,580 --> 00:28:54,280
If we spend the fund...

675
00:28:54,281 --> 00:28:56,249
without everyone's agreement,

676
00:28:56,584 --> 00:29:00,821
then, at a broader level,
we're taking money from a public fund.

677
00:29:00,822 --> 00:29:03,691
Even if it means taking some criticism,

678
00:29:03,692 --> 00:29:05,960
since we voluntarily chose
to take on this task,

679
00:29:05,961 --> 00:29:08,864
our goal should be to make
as much money as we can.

680
00:29:08,865 --> 00:29:11,131
I don't want us to be too risk-averse.

681
00:29:11,132 --> 00:29:13,367
We'll only become
more and more incentivized...

682
00:29:13,368 --> 00:29:14,468
to avoid taking risks.

683
00:29:14,469 --> 00:29:16,570
It's not just about avoiding risks.

684
00:29:16,571 --> 00:29:17,838
It could be just off-limits.

685
00:29:17,839 --> 00:29:19,807
To spend 500 dollars,

686
00:29:19,808 --> 00:29:21,609
we do need to follow the proper procedure,

687
00:29:21,610 --> 00:29:23,878
but I think it's a matter of the leader's
ability to convince others.

688
00:29:23,879 --> 00:29:27,114
If spending 500 dollars
doesn't really hurt the group,

689
00:29:27,115 --> 00:29:28,616
the leader or we...

690
00:29:28,617 --> 00:29:31,418
can convince everyone
that it was worth it.

691
00:29:31,419 --> 00:29:33,521
We don't have to miss 8,000 dollars.

692
00:29:47,469 --> 00:29:50,604
- It's raining so much.
- We're going to get completely soaked.

693
00:29:50,605 --> 00:29:52,773
- Was that lightning?
- That was lightning.

694
00:29:55,677 --> 00:29:56,644
Are they coming back?

695
00:29:56,912 --> 00:29:58,145
Are they coming?

696
00:29:59,400 --> 00:30:01,468
Money has been deducted
from the community fund.

697
00:30:01,983 --> 00:30:04,017
Money has been deducted
from the community fund.

698
00:30:09,925 --> 00:30:10,858
Why?

699
00:30:11,726 --> 00:30:13,227
What are they doing?

700
00:30:13,228 --> 00:30:14,595
We can't eat fried chicken anymore.

701
00:30:14,763 --> 00:30:18,165
Some money was taken out
of the community fund out of the blue.

702
00:30:18,433 --> 00:30:19,566
I was shocked.

703
00:30:19,835 --> 00:30:21,235
They're spending money.

704
00:30:21,236 --> 00:30:24,071
Something went wrong,
so they're dealing with it.

705
00:30:24,072 --> 00:30:26,241
That balance could drop fast.

706
00:30:26,242 --> 00:30:28,008
I never thought...

707
00:30:28,009 --> 00:30:30,311
it would drop that much, though.

708
00:30:30,488 --> 00:30:33,157
If the community fund
and my account both hit zero,

709
00:30:33,182 --> 00:30:35,282
we could actually starve.

710
00:30:35,283 --> 00:30:37,051
That's when it first hit me.

711
00:30:38,086 --> 00:30:41,021
(Five minutes ago)

712
00:30:46,428 --> 00:30:48,262
Could you make the call as leader?

713
00:30:48,263 --> 00:30:51,232
The leader is the one...

714
00:30:51,533 --> 00:30:53,968
who decides how to tax
that money later anyway.

715
00:30:53,969 --> 00:30:56,904
She's in charge
of distribution regardless.

716
00:30:56,905 --> 00:30:57,972
In that case,

717
00:30:58,307 --> 00:31:00,141
we can forfeit our share...

718
00:31:00,142 --> 00:31:02,409
- That's what I mean.
- to cover the 500 dollars.

719
00:31:02,410 --> 00:31:03,878
- I'm fine taking less.
- If we can justify it.

720
00:31:03,879 --> 00:31:05,446
- Count me in.
- I'm good with that.

721
00:31:05,447 --> 00:31:06,780
Let's give up our cut.

722
00:31:06,781 --> 00:31:08,281
We'll cover it.

723
00:31:08,450 --> 00:31:10,384
Reaching a compromise wasn't easy.

724
00:31:10,385 --> 00:31:13,287
I felt setting a precedent...

725
00:31:13,288 --> 00:31:14,889
of dipping into the community fund...

726
00:31:14,890 --> 00:31:17,825
without unanimous consent
was inherently wrong.

727
00:31:17,826 --> 00:31:19,260
It wasn't about the amount.

728
00:31:19,261 --> 00:31:21,896
It was about how we treat the fund itself.

729
00:31:21,897 --> 00:31:24,081
Even if we made a profit using it,

730
00:31:24,106 --> 00:31:26,375
we had to agree to share everything...

731
00:31:26,400 --> 00:31:28,703
exclusively with the rest
of the residents.

732
00:31:28,704 --> 00:31:30,604
I believed that would give us...

733
00:31:30,605 --> 00:31:34,876
a fair justification
for using the shared money.

734
00:31:34,877 --> 00:31:38,712
We agreed on that and went ahead.

735
00:31:40,148 --> 00:31:40,981
Let's do this!

736
00:31:41,483 --> 00:31:43,217
- All right!
- Let's go!

737
00:31:43,719 --> 00:31:44,718
This is...

738
00:31:45,954 --> 00:31:47,521
I'm so nervous.

739
00:31:47,522 --> 00:31:48,489
Oh boy.

740
00:31:48,490 --> 00:31:51,091
If this is wrong, we take a hint.

741
00:31:53,361 --> 00:31:54,528
Thirteen thousand.

742
00:31:54,529 --> 00:31:56,530
(Their estimate: 13,000)

743
00:31:59,901 --> 00:32:00,801
Incorrect.

744
00:32:04,906 --> 00:32:06,006
How...

745
00:32:07,342 --> 00:32:08,309
I know.

746
00:32:11,146 --> 00:32:13,214
Let's take the hint now.

747
00:32:14,916 --> 00:32:15,983
We'll use a hint.

748
00:32:17,052 --> 00:32:18,520
- What a waste.
- Our reward gets cut in half.

749
00:32:18,820 --> 00:32:19,820
This is so frustrating.

750
00:32:20,188 --> 00:32:22,789
Look. There are 25 districts in Seoul.

751
00:32:22,790 --> 00:32:23,824
Meaning...

752
00:32:24,225 --> 00:32:25,892
Benjamin will crunch the numbers.

753
00:32:26,294 --> 00:32:27,961
We'd be lost without him.

754
00:32:27,962 --> 00:32:30,063
Good thing we brought him along.

755
00:32:30,875 --> 00:32:33,176
Our answer is 11,760.

756
00:32:33,201 --> 00:32:34,168
Incorrect.

757
00:32:34,169 --> 00:32:35,402
(Their second attempt fails.)

758
00:32:36,204 --> 00:32:37,838
Would you like to try again?

759
00:32:38,907 --> 00:32:40,875
Money has been deducted
from the community fund.

760
00:32:40,876 --> 00:32:43,010
(Money is deducted for the second time.)

761
00:32:45,046 --> 00:32:47,314
What on earth did they buy?

762
00:32:47,683 --> 00:32:49,250
What are they spending it on?

763
00:32:49,618 --> 00:32:50,761
What's going on over there?

764
00:32:50,786 --> 00:32:53,921
Our answer is 10,640.

765
00:32:53,922 --> 00:32:54,822
Incorrect.

766
00:32:54,823 --> 00:32:56,924
(Their third attempt fails.)

767
00:32:57,092 --> 00:32:58,827
Would you like to try again?

768
00:32:59,561 --> 00:33:01,595
Money has been deducted
from the community fund.

769
00:33:01,596 --> 00:33:02,596
(Money is deducted for the third time.)

770
00:33:02,597 --> 00:33:03,998
Hold on.

771
00:33:04,565 --> 00:33:06,766
Wait. What are they doing?

772
00:33:07,168 --> 00:33:10,137
If she burns cash and fails on top of it,

773
00:33:10,138 --> 00:33:13,508
she has zero chance of getting reelected.

774
00:33:14,175 --> 00:33:15,275
This is our last shot.

775
00:33:15,300 --> 00:33:16,711
We don't have a choice.

776
00:33:16,878 --> 00:33:18,044
I'm sorry, everyone.

777
00:33:18,045 --> 00:33:19,246
I'm really sorry.

778
00:33:19,681 --> 00:33:21,348
It's 9,626.

779
00:33:21,950 --> 00:33:23,017
Incorrect.

780
00:33:25,086 --> 00:33:26,421
Worst-case scenario.

781
00:33:27,355 --> 00:33:31,625
(Cannot retry due to insufficient funds
under 500 dollars)

782
00:33:32,094 --> 00:33:34,129
- How could we miss that?
- What was the answer?

783
00:33:35,230 --> 00:33:36,530
(Level 5 answer: 8,534)

784
00:33:36,531 --> 00:33:37,364
In the 8,000s?

785
00:33:37,499 --> 00:33:39,033
It was that low?

786
00:33:39,467 --> 00:33:41,368
It was in the tier below.

787
00:33:42,171 --> 00:33:44,472
One bracket off. That's infuriating.

788
00:33:46,841 --> 00:33:50,076
Your total prize money earned today...

789
00:33:50,612 --> 00:33:52,212
is 4,000 dollars.

790
00:33:52,657 --> 00:33:55,058
- Total amount spent today...
- Oh no.

791
00:33:55,083 --> 00:33:56,983
is 1,500 dollars.

792
00:33:59,654 --> 00:34:01,255
Let's go face the music.

793
00:34:01,256 --> 00:34:03,023
Time to take our punishment.

794
00:34:04,626 --> 00:34:06,227
Glad the rain stopped.

795
00:34:07,328 --> 00:34:09,195
Is 400 dollars worth four coffees?

796
00:34:10,231 --> 00:34:11,908
- You guys didn't get wet.
- Completely dry.

797
00:34:11,933 --> 00:34:12,977
Not a drop on them.

798
00:34:13,468 --> 00:34:14,434
Don't worry about it.

799
00:34:14,435 --> 00:34:15,302
It's all good.

800
00:34:15,303 --> 00:34:17,671
- Was it indoors?
- Yeah, it was.

801
00:34:19,875 --> 00:34:21,108
Good work, you guys.

802
00:34:21,743 --> 00:34:24,478
- We're so sorry.
- You look exhausted.

803
00:34:25,013 --> 00:34:26,947
We'll explain everything.

804
00:34:26,948 --> 00:34:28,849
We're dying to know, though.

805
00:34:28,850 --> 00:34:31,018
Did retries cost 500 dollars?

806
00:34:31,252 --> 00:34:33,486
- That was our guess.
- We'll fill you in.

807
00:34:33,487 --> 00:34:34,421
Tell us later.

808
00:34:34,422 --> 00:34:36,189
It was for the retries.

809
00:34:36,190 --> 00:34:37,390
Here's what happened...

810
00:34:37,794 --> 00:34:39,827
Mostly, I was just curious.

811
00:34:39,828 --> 00:34:42,062
What was the task?

812
00:34:42,063 --> 00:34:44,131
How did they spend the shared fund?

813
00:34:44,132 --> 00:34:47,701
We never agreed to touch that money.

814
00:34:47,702 --> 00:34:51,071
Not knowing what happened
makes me even angrier.

815
00:34:59,380 --> 00:35:01,081
Look at them. They look so defeated.

816
00:35:01,316 --> 00:35:03,084
Good job, guys. Must've been brutal.

817
00:35:04,519 --> 00:35:06,119
First off,

818
00:35:06,120 --> 00:35:08,856
let's explain...

819
00:35:09,357 --> 00:35:11,358
how the game worked.

820
00:35:11,359 --> 00:35:14,328
And why we ended up dipping into the fund.

821
00:35:14,763 --> 00:35:17,998
Our economic activity...

822
00:35:17,999 --> 00:35:20,634
was a trivia challenge.

823
00:35:20,635 --> 00:35:23,003
The goal was to guess
the closest to the actual figure.

824
00:35:23,805 --> 00:35:25,539
The first question was the easiest.

825
00:35:25,540 --> 00:35:28,010
Three ten-liter food waste bags...

826
00:35:28,035 --> 00:35:30,043
- A movie ticket...
- How much did it cost?

827
00:35:30,044 --> 00:35:31,011
Practical, real-world stuff.

828
00:35:31,012 --> 00:35:33,380
The third question was
about a conscript's pay.

829
00:35:33,381 --> 00:35:35,883
Total savings accumulated by discharge.

830
00:35:35,884 --> 00:35:38,052
What percentage of victims are female?

831
00:35:38,053 --> 00:35:40,020
Out of 25,000 victims, 21,000 are women?

832
00:35:40,021 --> 00:35:41,522
That was jarring.

833
00:35:42,123 --> 00:35:43,623
That stat caught me off guard.

834
00:35:43,624 --> 00:35:44,558
Seriously?

835
00:35:44,559 --> 00:35:46,793
Nobody was expected to know that
off the top of their head.

836
00:35:46,794 --> 00:35:49,730
Unless you specifically studied it,
no way you'd get it.

837
00:35:49,731 --> 00:35:51,265
Even lawyers or judges wouldn't know that.

838
00:35:51,266 --> 00:35:53,800
How are we supposed to know
exact crime victim stats?

839
00:35:53,801 --> 00:35:54,735
Oh, please.

840
00:35:54,736 --> 00:35:55,769
That's ridiculous.

841
00:35:57,972 --> 00:36:00,507
If I'd gone, I could've actually helped.

842
00:36:00,508 --> 00:36:02,910
Having a sense of practical reality...

843
00:36:02,911 --> 00:36:04,911
isn't some rare superpower.

844
00:36:04,912 --> 00:36:06,514
It just means knowing...

845
00:36:06,515 --> 00:36:09,182
everyday costs like trash bags
or movie tickets.

846
00:36:09,183 --> 00:36:11,285
When prepping for this show,

847
00:36:11,286 --> 00:36:12,986
I figured questions like that
would come up.

848
00:36:12,987 --> 00:36:16,356
Politicians always get tested on basics
like minimum wage...

849
00:36:16,357 --> 00:36:17,490
or base taxi fare.

850
00:36:17,491 --> 00:36:20,093
You naturally pick these things up...

851
00:36:20,094 --> 00:36:24,030
if you're living a normal everyday life.

852
00:36:24,031 --> 00:36:27,200
I'm sure they tried hard
and did what they could.

853
00:36:27,201 --> 00:36:29,636
But looking back...

854
00:36:29,637 --> 00:36:31,938
at how they reasoned through it...

855
00:36:31,939 --> 00:36:34,407
and reached those figures,

856
00:36:34,408 --> 00:36:36,343
they relied heavily on personal bias.

857
00:36:36,344 --> 00:36:38,345
Military pay.

858
00:36:38,446 --> 00:36:40,514
I served as a professional soldier.

859
00:36:40,515 --> 00:36:42,348
I definitely had the upper hand...

860
00:36:42,349 --> 00:36:44,686
when it came to that specific question.

861
00:36:45,053 --> 00:36:48,121
Number four was
about single-person households.

862
00:36:48,122 --> 00:36:49,958
The only one we got right.

863
00:36:50,625 --> 00:36:51,725
You nailed that one?

864
00:36:51,726 --> 00:36:53,660
By sheer luck, our estimate
fell within the margin,

865
00:36:53,661 --> 00:36:55,062
so we took home 4,000 dollars.

866
00:36:55,063 --> 00:36:56,229
You're in the green!

867
00:36:56,230 --> 00:36:57,531
They turned a profit.

868
00:36:57,966 --> 00:37:00,033
You didn't just burn cash.
You came out ahead.

869
00:37:00,034 --> 00:37:02,235
- We profited.
- You made more than you spent.

870
00:37:02,236 --> 00:37:04,437
The key thing here is...

871
00:37:04,438 --> 00:37:06,306
Superman and I...

872
00:37:06,307 --> 00:37:09,075
- didn't see an issue...
- Right.

873
00:37:09,477 --> 00:37:12,412
with tapping into the shared fund.

874
00:37:12,413 --> 00:37:13,413
Exactly.

875
00:37:13,414 --> 00:37:15,882
But Ted and the leader pointed out...

876
00:37:15,883 --> 00:37:19,085
that the others hadn't signed off on it.

877
00:37:19,354 --> 00:37:21,722
Speaking as the leader
who had to make the call,

878
00:37:21,723 --> 00:37:25,592
regardless of how much
we could potentially win,

879
00:37:25,593 --> 00:37:28,862
touching the community fund...

880
00:37:29,031 --> 00:37:32,099
felt like crossing a line.

881
00:37:32,100 --> 00:37:34,534
So as a compromise,

882
00:37:34,535 --> 00:37:35,869
we decided...

883
00:37:35,870 --> 00:37:39,706
that our crew wouldn't take
a single dime of the prize.

884
00:37:39,841 --> 00:37:43,311
We agreed that 100 percent
would go back to the community...

885
00:37:43,845 --> 00:37:45,279
and only then did we spend it.

886
00:37:45,280 --> 00:37:47,047
We refused to touch that money...

887
00:37:47,048 --> 00:37:49,850
until everyone on the team
agreed to that term.

888
00:37:50,152 --> 00:37:51,352
All of you agreed to that?

889
00:37:51,353 --> 00:37:52,619
Yeah. Everyone in our group.

890
00:37:52,620 --> 00:37:53,754
Wow, really?

891
00:37:54,055 --> 00:37:55,655
In any case,

892
00:37:55,656 --> 00:37:59,059
I apologize for spending shared funds
without consulting everyone first.

893
00:37:59,060 --> 00:38:00,927
- You did great, though.
- Great job.

894
00:38:00,928 --> 00:38:02,596
- Well done.
- Seriously.

895
00:38:02,839 --> 00:38:03,534
You did great.

896
00:38:03,559 --> 00:38:06,066
Answering five questions felt
like it took five whole hours.

897
00:38:06,067 --> 00:38:07,410
They really agonized over every pick.

898
00:38:07,435 --> 00:38:09,704
- They were super careful.
- Must've been stressful.

899
00:38:11,673 --> 00:38:13,507
So they want us to divide the prize?

900
00:38:13,508 --> 00:38:15,809
The leader has to visit
the Community Center,

901
00:38:15,810 --> 00:38:17,077
distribute the payout,

902
00:38:17,078 --> 00:38:19,347
- and collect taxes for the fund.
- I'll transfer it all back to the fund.

903
00:38:21,049 --> 00:38:24,484
Not one of the six pushed back?

904
00:38:24,485 --> 00:38:26,587
Someone could've easily objected
to giving up their cut.

905
00:38:26,588 --> 00:38:28,422
They seemed completely genuine.

906
00:38:28,689 --> 00:38:30,123
If that was an act...

907
00:38:31,125 --> 00:38:33,626
You could tell they gave it everything.

908
00:38:33,928 --> 00:38:36,229
They earned that money fair and square.

909
00:38:36,230 --> 00:38:38,565
They busted their tails out there.

910
00:38:38,566 --> 00:38:40,734
And yet they're apologizing
for not bringing back more.

911
00:38:40,735 --> 00:38:42,458
It felt performative,

912
00:38:42,482 --> 00:38:45,272
almost like they were trying too hard...

913
00:38:45,273 --> 00:38:47,340
to prove what good people they are.

914
00:38:47,341 --> 00:38:49,643
The whole dynamic felt weird to me.

915
00:38:50,345 --> 00:38:51,545
Hold on.

916
00:38:51,546 --> 00:38:53,180
Everyone who went on the mission.

917
00:38:53,181 --> 00:38:55,015
Can I talk to you guys
over here for a sec?

918
00:38:55,016 --> 00:38:56,517
We have a problem.

919
00:38:56,518 --> 00:38:57,551
Is something wrong?

920
00:38:57,552 --> 00:38:59,887
Be right back.
Sounds like she needs to talk.

921
00:38:59,888 --> 00:39:02,356
- See ya.
- Always something with this game.

922
00:39:03,057 --> 00:39:06,893
The rule says payouts must be
in 1,000-dollar increments.

923
00:39:07,071 --> 00:39:08,838
I'll transfer it all back to the fund.

924
00:39:08,863 --> 00:39:11,198
(Ten minutes ago)

925
00:39:11,966 --> 00:39:14,234
I had to set the tax rate.

926
00:39:14,235 --> 00:39:16,771
But I wasn't clear
if I could tax people individually...

927
00:39:16,872 --> 00:39:20,840
or if a flat tax rate
applied across the board.

928
00:39:21,042 --> 00:39:23,076
(The leader can decide
distribution amounts,)

929
00:39:23,101 --> 00:39:26,437
(but cannot withhold distribution
entirely.)

930
00:39:26,614 --> 00:39:29,174
(Taxes are levied equally
against personal funds...)

931
00:39:29,175 --> 00:39:31,851
(after the initial distribution
is completed.)

932
00:39:32,861 --> 00:39:36,063
Just hand out 1,000-dollar
chunks at random...

933
00:39:36,258 --> 00:39:37,524
and tax us at 100 percent.

934
00:39:37,525 --> 00:39:39,494
Doesn't everyone get taxed
at the exact same rate?

935
00:39:40,962 --> 00:39:43,697
The tax applies
to total personal balances.

936
00:39:43,698 --> 00:39:45,499
Some have 700 dollars, others have 3,000.

937
00:39:45,500 --> 00:39:48,468
If the tax rate is 100 percent,

938
00:39:48,469 --> 00:39:50,238
everyone loses every penny
they have to the fund.

939
00:39:50,405 --> 00:39:53,073
You see the problem?
Balances aren't equal.

940
00:39:53,074 --> 00:39:55,642
It wipes out total existing savings.

941
00:39:55,643 --> 00:39:56,645
- Oh.
- Right.

942
00:39:56,646 --> 00:39:59,012
It drains what we currently hold.

943
00:39:59,013 --> 00:40:01,614
I thought we could bypass distribution
and dump it directly into the shared fund.

944
00:40:01,615 --> 00:40:03,817
I assumed we could skip that step.

945
00:40:04,252 --> 00:40:08,421
Then we have to distribute
the 4,000 dollars across four people.

946
00:40:08,422 --> 00:40:10,034
Since it has to be
in 1,000-dollar increments.

947
00:40:10,059 --> 00:40:11,992
We thought it was way simpler than this.

948
00:40:12,226 --> 00:40:14,595
Still, a promise is a promise.

949
00:40:14,596 --> 00:40:16,229
Right. Should we take zero...

950
00:40:16,230 --> 00:40:17,830
and split it equally among them?

951
00:40:17,831 --> 00:40:21,467
But we have to pick four people.

952
00:40:21,468 --> 00:40:22,602
Should we draw lots?

953
00:40:22,603 --> 00:40:25,004
Let's have the other six do it.

954
00:40:25,005 --> 00:40:26,606
Personally,

955
00:40:26,607 --> 00:40:29,976
I think we should give it
to whoever has a zero balance.

956
00:40:30,346 --> 00:40:33,356
But if they see that as a weakness...

957
00:40:33,381 --> 00:40:35,249
and feel uncomfortable speaking up,

958
00:40:35,584 --> 00:40:38,518
they don't have to.

959
00:40:38,519 --> 00:40:39,829
Would that make things worse?

960
00:40:39,854 --> 00:40:43,356
Let's make it clear
this won't be held against anyone,

961
00:40:43,925 --> 00:40:45,459
then bring them in one by one.

962
00:40:45,460 --> 00:40:47,027
All they have to say is
whether they need the money.

963
00:40:47,028 --> 00:40:49,030
That way, nobody has
to put themselves on blast.

964
00:40:49,297 --> 00:40:51,298
Right. I can wait in here.

965
00:40:51,299 --> 00:40:53,267
If you're the only one who knows,

966
00:40:53,268 --> 00:40:57,671
that keeps the fallout to a minimum.

967
00:40:57,672 --> 00:41:00,007
It's up to the leader,

968
00:41:00,008 --> 00:41:03,009
but I really don't like the idea
of handpicking four people...

969
00:41:03,010 --> 00:41:06,880
and handing out cash
like it's some handout.

970
00:41:07,315 --> 00:41:09,483
- Let's not make unilateral calls.
- Agreed.

971
00:41:09,484 --> 00:41:11,719
- That's crucial.
- Let's just be transparent with everyone.

972
00:41:11,953 --> 00:41:14,321
(Personal Funds Status)

973
00:41:14,322 --> 00:41:16,690
(2,700 dollars,
2,100 dollars, 2,100 dollars)

974
00:41:16,691 --> 00:41:18,379
(1,700 dollars, 1,700 dollars,
1,700 dollars)

975
00:41:18,404 --> 00:41:19,626
(1,400 dollars, 1,400 dollars)

976
00:41:19,627 --> 00:41:23,030
(700 dollars, 700 dollars, 400 dollars,
0 dollars)

977
00:41:23,564 --> 00:41:27,567
My initial plan was to withhold
distribution entirely...

978
00:41:27,568 --> 00:41:30,004
and pour everything back
into the community fund.

979
00:41:30,005 --> 00:41:32,172
We were just going
to write zero on our slips.

980
00:41:32,173 --> 00:41:34,941
But payouts have to be made
in 1,000-dollar increments.

981
00:41:34,942 --> 00:41:37,677
- Minimum unit.
- Non-negotiable.

982
00:41:37,846 --> 00:41:41,648
So I have to split this
between four people.

983
00:41:41,716 --> 00:41:44,618
But as we agreed earlier,

984
00:41:44,920 --> 00:41:47,387
to take accountability
for using the shared fund,

985
00:41:47,388 --> 00:41:49,189
our team won't take a dime.

986
00:41:49,190 --> 00:41:50,725
That was the deal.

987
00:41:51,859 --> 00:41:56,296
Everyone's financial situation
is different right now.

988
00:41:56,297 --> 00:41:58,531
Some took part in the debate, some didn't.

989
00:41:58,532 --> 00:42:01,034
I assume the gap between balances
is pretty wide.

990
00:42:01,035 --> 00:42:04,704
If your funds are running low,

991
00:42:04,839 --> 00:42:07,374
I'll be waiting in that room.

992
00:42:07,375 --> 00:42:09,876
Just step in privately
and let me know you need it.

993
00:42:09,877 --> 00:42:11,477
I'll keep it strictly anonymous...

994
00:42:11,478 --> 00:42:13,713
and distribute it accordingly.

995
00:42:14,081 --> 00:42:17,717
Honestly, that's the fundamental flaw
of welfare systems.

996
00:42:17,852 --> 00:42:19,595
When people in need ask for help,

997
00:42:19,620 --> 00:42:21,755
the biggest barrier...

998
00:42:21,756 --> 00:42:25,091
is being forced to prove
how poor they are just to qualify.

999
00:42:25,092 --> 00:42:26,927
And that's essentially
what they're making us do.

1000
00:42:26,928 --> 00:42:29,496
It made me realize
how naive they still are.

1001
00:42:29,521 --> 00:42:32,456
At the same time, I figured
I might as well go in and ask.

1002
00:42:32,633 --> 00:42:34,367
We feel terrible.

1003
00:42:34,368 --> 00:42:38,439
Dropping the bomb that while you guys
were just sitting here...

1004
00:42:38,472 --> 00:42:41,741
minding your own business,
you're about to get taxed.

1005
00:42:41,742 --> 00:42:43,242
- We're sorry.
- Really sorry.

1006
00:42:43,243 --> 00:42:46,045
If someone's balance is at zero,

1007
00:42:46,914 --> 00:42:49,449
they can't be taxed anyway.

1008
00:42:49,450 --> 00:42:51,584
Right. A zero balance means...

1009
00:42:51,585 --> 00:42:52,986
there's nothing to take.

1010
00:42:53,154 --> 00:42:55,055
Meaning...

1011
00:42:55,489 --> 00:42:56,890
Wait, what are you saying?

1012
00:42:56,891 --> 00:42:59,626
Honestly, the fairest way...

1013
00:42:59,627 --> 00:43:01,326
- is just drawing lots.
- That's cleaner.

1014
00:43:01,327 --> 00:43:04,974
Even if you keep it "anonymous"...

1015
00:43:04,999 --> 00:43:06,140
you still have to expose yourself.

1016
00:43:06,165 --> 00:43:08,000
The leader still finds out.

1017
00:43:08,001 --> 00:43:10,204
Drawing lots is way better.

1018
00:43:10,637 --> 00:43:13,073
- Can I ask something?
- Go ahead.

1019
00:43:13,074 --> 00:43:16,542
Can someone actually survive
with zero funds?

1020
00:43:19,447 --> 00:43:21,348
That's going to catch up to you
eventually.

1021
00:43:21,549 --> 00:43:23,116
I think so too.

1022
00:43:23,117 --> 00:43:25,353
They wouldn't eliminate someone
purely for hitting zero, right?

1023
00:43:25,620 --> 00:43:27,788
- It could happen.
- They haven't explicitly said...

1024
00:43:27,789 --> 00:43:30,223
being broke equals elimination,

1025
00:43:30,224 --> 00:43:32,293
but who knows what twists are coming.

1026
00:43:32,426 --> 00:43:35,361
Can I chime in?

1027
00:43:36,197 --> 00:43:38,212
Why should your team be left out
after working so hard?

1028
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:40,167
Look at it this way.

1029
00:43:40,308 --> 00:43:42,577
I appreciate you looking out for us,

1030
00:43:42,637 --> 00:43:45,738
but none of us should
take that for granted.

1031
00:43:46,741 --> 00:43:49,043
You guys busted your tails
to earn this money.

1032
00:43:49,477 --> 00:43:51,911
Why should we pocket that cash
all to ourselves?

1033
00:43:51,912 --> 00:43:53,947
I say we put everyone in the pool...

1034
00:43:54,115 --> 00:43:57,952
and make sure those running low
get a shot at it.

1035
00:43:58,819 --> 00:44:00,787
That's far healthier for the whole house.

1036
00:44:00,788 --> 00:44:03,224
Openly admitting you're broke
basically confesses...

1037
00:44:03,691 --> 00:44:05,325
that you lost yesterday's debate.

1038
00:44:05,326 --> 00:44:07,461
You're giving away intel
about your position.

1039
00:44:08,396 --> 00:44:10,564
It signals that you lost.

1040
00:44:10,565 --> 00:44:11,431
Exactly.

1041
00:44:12,300 --> 00:44:13,300
- Let's all do it.
- Let's all draw.

1042
00:44:13,301 --> 00:44:15,035
Let's draw lots together.

1043
00:44:15,703 --> 00:44:18,171
If only the remaining six draw,

1044
00:44:18,172 --> 00:44:20,373
I could easily end up
on the chopping block.

1045
00:44:20,374 --> 00:44:22,842
This might sound strange,
but count me out.

1046
00:44:22,843 --> 00:44:24,677
This sits wrong with me.

1047
00:44:24,678 --> 00:44:25,712
Don't say that.

1048
00:44:26,081 --> 00:44:27,213
No, really.

1049
00:44:27,214 --> 00:44:29,883
We made a pact among ourselves.

1050
00:44:29,884 --> 00:44:32,686
- We swore we wouldn't take anything.
- That was just between you six.

1051
00:44:32,820 --> 00:44:35,255
But the shared fund came
from everyone's taxes.

1052
00:44:35,256 --> 00:44:37,725
- I disagree.
- This doesn't sit right.

1053
00:44:38,059 --> 00:44:39,927
I stand by my plan.

1054
00:44:39,928 --> 00:44:41,861
Those in financial need
should get the money.

1055
00:44:41,862 --> 00:44:45,332
But it has to be handled with privacy.

1056
00:44:45,333 --> 00:44:48,035
I side with Dark Knight.

1057
00:44:49,470 --> 00:44:50,437
Let's all draw.

1058
00:44:50,871 --> 00:44:53,606
- Right.
- That keeps it fair and clean.

1059
00:44:53,607 --> 00:44:54,974
Nobody ends up feeling
indebted to anyone else.

1060
00:44:54,975 --> 00:44:56,498
We established this ground rule...

1061
00:44:56,522 --> 00:44:59,177
because we needed a moral justification...

1062
00:44:59,178 --> 00:45:00,613
for tapping into the fund.

1063
00:45:00,614 --> 00:45:02,281
As leader,

1064
00:45:02,282 --> 00:45:05,118
I don't believe we should discard
our principles so easily.

1065
00:45:05,119 --> 00:45:06,986
I understand where you're coming from,

1066
00:45:06,987 --> 00:45:08,621
but I ask that you respect our decision.

1067
00:45:08,622 --> 00:45:10,923
Wait, no.

1068
00:45:10,924 --> 00:45:12,592
- Hang on.
- No?

1069
00:45:12,593 --> 00:45:13,626
Sorry for digging in my heels.

1070
00:45:13,627 --> 00:45:15,939
I think this is the first time
I've ever stood my ground like this.

1071
00:45:15,964 --> 00:45:17,398
At least draw a slip with us.

1072
00:45:18,265 --> 00:45:20,466
We can't do that.

1073
00:45:20,467 --> 00:45:23,269
- Once we bend our principles,
- Exactly.

1074
00:45:23,270 --> 00:45:25,637
setting rules in the future
becomes completely meaningless.

1075
00:45:25,638 --> 00:45:27,773
If I can add one thing,

1076
00:45:28,142 --> 00:45:30,276
I feel for Dark Knight,

1077
00:45:30,277 --> 00:45:32,212
but we need to back the leader on this.

1078
00:45:32,513 --> 00:45:34,147
It's deadlocked six against six anyway,

1079
00:45:34,148 --> 00:45:36,049
so it's the leader's call to make.

1080
00:45:36,050 --> 00:45:37,217
Fair enough. My bad.

1081
00:45:37,218 --> 00:45:39,486
- Don't apologize at all.
- Sorry about that.

1082
00:45:40,188 --> 00:45:43,489
The people who had it easiest today
were the ones lounging at home.

1083
00:45:43,490 --> 00:45:46,860
Yet they stayed totally silent.

1084
00:45:47,361 --> 00:45:49,228
Clearly, they just wanted the cash.

1085
00:45:49,229 --> 00:45:51,499
That's when my authoritarian side
flared up.

1086
00:45:51,665 --> 00:45:53,466
"Screw it. Everyone draws."

1087
00:45:53,467 --> 00:45:56,168
If I had forced my hand right then,

1088
00:45:56,169 --> 00:45:59,105
nobody could have faulted me for it.

1089
00:45:59,106 --> 00:46:02,608
In moments like that,
you need a touch of autocracy...

1090
00:46:03,110 --> 00:46:04,844
over diplomacy, consensus, or voting.

1091
00:46:05,779 --> 00:46:08,081
- Draw lots.
- Let's draw.

1092
00:46:08,315 --> 00:46:09,482
(Distributing prize pool
among 4 staying residents)

1093
00:46:09,483 --> 00:46:11,184
I'll run the draw.

1094
00:46:11,652 --> 00:46:13,687
Pick at random.

1095
00:46:20,895 --> 00:46:22,029
Want to trade slips?

1096
00:46:22,530 --> 00:46:24,798
No way. Why would anyone trade
after hearing that?

1097
00:46:24,799 --> 00:46:25,899
Open them up.

1098
00:46:26,700 --> 00:46:27,733
Winner.

1099
00:46:27,734 --> 00:46:29,902
- Genie.
- Genie got one.

1100
00:46:30,505 --> 00:46:31,372
I won.

1101
00:46:31,373 --> 00:46:32,839
- Michael won.
- Oh, wow.

1102
00:46:32,840 --> 00:46:33,907
Michael.

1103
00:46:34,642 --> 00:46:35,575
I won!

1104
00:46:35,576 --> 00:46:36,910
(Sugar with 1,700 dollars: Win)

1105
00:46:36,911 --> 00:46:37,844
Blank.

1106
00:46:37,845 --> 00:46:40,580
(Hippo with 700 dollars: Lose)

1107
00:46:40,714 --> 00:46:42,581
Blank.

1108
00:46:42,582 --> 00:46:44,650
(Nangja with 0 dollars: Lose)

1109
00:46:44,651 --> 00:46:45,718
I'm sorry.

1110
00:46:46,120 --> 00:46:48,822
(Dark Knight with 700 dollars: Win)

1111
00:46:51,692 --> 00:46:53,294
Level with us. You're broke, aren't you?

1112
00:46:54,295 --> 00:46:55,929
Who, me? Yeah, completely.

1113
00:46:55,930 --> 00:46:57,596
Dark Knight, you got one?

1114
00:46:57,597 --> 00:46:59,031
Please pass it to Hippo.

1115
00:46:59,032 --> 00:47:00,099
Seriously?

1116
00:47:01,669 --> 00:47:03,037
I don't need it.

1117
00:47:03,370 --> 00:47:04,337
All right.

1118
00:47:04,805 --> 00:47:06,606
Reallocations are permitted.

1119
00:47:06,607 --> 00:47:08,384
Since the official payout
hasn't finalized yet.

1120
00:47:08,409 --> 00:47:10,710
(Dark Knight transfers
1,000 dollars to Hippo.)

1121
00:47:10,711 --> 00:47:12,545
True to his name, what a knight.

1122
00:47:12,713 --> 00:47:13,948
You can repay me with a heart.

1123
00:47:14,949 --> 00:47:17,083
- Absolutely.
- You know where that vote is going.

1124
00:47:17,084 --> 00:47:20,020
True. Is Dark Knight sweeping
all the hearts today?

1125
00:47:20,021 --> 00:47:21,721
- Hands down.
- Doubt it.

1126
00:47:21,722 --> 00:47:24,091
- That was smooth.
- You really looked on edge, though.

1127
00:47:25,493 --> 00:47:27,294
All right, all settled.

1128
00:47:27,761 --> 00:47:29,462
If Dark Knight ran his numbers right,

1129
00:47:29,463 --> 00:47:32,031
he can't have much left himself.

1130
00:47:32,032 --> 00:47:34,267
Yet he handed it straight to Hippo.

1131
00:47:34,268 --> 00:47:37,670
I don't think she's strapped
for cash either.

1132
00:47:37,805 --> 00:47:39,773
It made me realize once again
how sharp she really is.

1133
00:47:39,774 --> 00:47:43,243
How did she signal her financial state
without me noticing?

1134
00:47:43,244 --> 00:47:44,911
Is she really that good at this game?

1135
00:47:44,912 --> 00:47:48,248
I felt backed into a corner,

1136
00:47:48,249 --> 00:47:51,317
so I pushed extra hard today.

1137
00:47:51,318 --> 00:47:53,620
I guess I made that too obvious.

1138
00:47:53,675 --> 00:47:55,208
But thanks to that,

1139
00:47:55,461 --> 00:47:57,129
in the end,

1140
00:47:58,392 --> 00:47:59,792
I got the cash passed to me.

1141
00:47:59,793 --> 00:48:02,194
I figured 1,000 dollars
could buy her loyalty.

1142
00:48:02,195 --> 00:48:04,097
Hippo was so desperate...

1143
00:48:04,098 --> 00:48:06,666
that giving her 1,000 dollars...

1144
00:48:06,667 --> 00:48:09,202
felt like a surefire way to win her over.

1145
00:48:09,203 --> 00:48:12,705
She seems pretty guarded around guys.

1146
00:48:12,706 --> 00:48:17,744
I gave her that money
to break down the wall between us.

1147
00:48:17,945 --> 00:48:20,847
Even if she unmasks me as a spy later,

1148
00:48:20,848 --> 00:48:22,982
she'll stay in my corner...

1149
00:48:22,983 --> 00:48:25,618
because I stepped up
when she needed it most.

1150
00:48:26,196 --> 00:48:27,296
Moving right along,

1151
00:48:27,321 --> 00:48:29,889
I need to set the tax rate.

1152
00:48:29,890 --> 00:48:32,091
How much to pull into the fund.

1153
00:48:32,092 --> 00:48:34,294
Since revenue was low,
we should tighten our belts.

1154
00:48:34,295 --> 00:48:37,329
Yeah. Fiscal conservatism is the move.

1155
00:48:37,330 --> 00:48:39,466
- Lean operations in a stagnant economy.
- Exactly, low growth.

1156
00:48:40,401 --> 00:48:43,469
The taxes we levy today...

1157
00:48:43,470 --> 00:48:46,640
are the seed money we need
to generate income tomorrow.

1158
00:48:46,641 --> 00:48:48,241
- Exactly.
- Right.

1159
00:48:48,242 --> 00:48:50,810
That's why setting the rate
is such a tightrope walk.

1160
00:48:50,811 --> 00:48:51,978
Completely agree.

1161
00:48:51,979 --> 00:48:54,080
Ballpark estimate,

1162
00:48:54,081 --> 00:48:58,451
at 10 percent, we pull in
around 1,800 to 1,900 dollars.

1163
00:48:58,452 --> 00:49:01,687
If we collect 1,800
at the minimum bracket,

1164
00:49:01,688 --> 00:49:04,791
we're left with barely 800 dollars...

1165
00:49:04,792 --> 00:49:06,359
after deducting the leader's
1,000-dollar stipend.

1166
00:49:06,360 --> 00:49:08,327
That's nowhere near enough...

1167
00:49:08,328 --> 00:49:10,897
to place meaningful bets or keep us fed.

1168
00:49:11,098 --> 00:49:12,698
- How about 20 percent?
- Twenty percent.

1169
00:49:12,699 --> 00:49:15,268
- Works for me.
- Sounds reasonable.

1170
00:49:15,269 --> 00:49:16,636
- Solid.
- Deal.

1171
00:49:16,637 --> 00:49:18,805
All right. I'll go lock it in.

1172
00:49:19,039 --> 00:49:20,272
- Let's do it!
- Time to eat soon.

1173
00:49:20,643 --> 00:49:22,709
Statecraft is exhausting.

1174
00:49:22,710 --> 00:49:23,843
It really is.

1175
00:49:23,844 --> 00:49:25,578
(The Community Center)

1176
00:49:27,214 --> 00:49:31,417
Genie, Michael, Sugar, and Hippo...

1177
00:49:31,418 --> 00:49:33,253
each receive 1,000 dollars.

1178
00:49:33,254 --> 00:49:39,025
(Personal Funds Status)

1179
00:49:39,026 --> 00:49:40,993
(3,100 dollars, 2,700 dollars,
2,700 dollars, 2,100 dollars)

1180
00:49:40,994 --> 00:49:42,195
(1,700 dollars, 1,700 dollars,
1,700 dollars)

1181
00:49:42,196 --> 00:49:43,896
(1,400 dollars, 1,400 dollars,
1,400 dollars)

1182
00:49:43,897 --> 00:49:47,200
(700 dollars, 0 dollars)

1183
00:49:47,201 --> 00:49:51,404
Today's tax rate is set at 20 percent.

1184
00:49:51,840 --> 00:49:55,297
Twenty percent of all personal balances...

1185
00:49:55,321 --> 00:49:57,344
has been collected
into the community fund.

1186
00:49:59,013 --> 00:50:00,647
Real food tonight!

1187
00:50:00,648 --> 00:50:03,216
Ramyeon or fried chicken.

1188
00:50:03,217 --> 00:50:04,451
What a luxury problem to have.

1189
00:50:04,786 --> 00:50:06,653
The best kind of problem right now.

1190
00:50:07,855 --> 00:50:11,191
My jaw dropped when I saw
how much prize money I had left.

1191
00:50:11,992 --> 00:50:13,626
I hadn't been paying
attention to it at all.

1192
00:50:13,627 --> 00:50:14,728
Post-tax reality check.

1193
00:50:14,729 --> 00:50:16,629
I have nothing left. Totally wiped out.

1194
00:50:16,630 --> 00:50:18,731
(Nangja, 0 dollars)

1195
00:50:18,732 --> 00:50:21,801
Like you said, this really is a society.

1196
00:50:22,736 --> 00:50:25,239
Next thing you know,
we'll be drafting a constitution.

1197
00:50:26,073 --> 00:50:27,941
What if we order...

1198
00:50:27,942 --> 00:50:30,043
fried chicken and this?

1199
00:50:30,044 --> 00:50:31,211
Sounds like a plan.

1200
00:50:31,212 --> 00:50:32,345
We'll order fried chicken.

1201
00:50:32,346 --> 00:50:33,680
- Sounds good.
- Yes!

1202
00:50:33,681 --> 00:50:34,815
Fried chicken and beer.

1203
00:50:34,816 --> 00:50:36,282
Your order is confirmed.

1204
00:50:36,418 --> 00:50:38,184
Payment will be deducted
from the community fund.

1205
00:50:38,185 --> 00:50:41,020
You'll be notified
once your order arrives.

1206
00:50:41,288 --> 00:50:42,622
That's pretty steep.

1207
00:50:43,323 --> 00:50:46,793
If we were allowed to use
our own cash for this...

1208
00:50:46,794 --> 00:50:47,993
We'd be fighting nonstop.

1209
00:50:47,994 --> 00:50:51,130
Imagine someone rich
slurping cup ramyeon...

1210
00:50:51,265 --> 00:50:52,598
right in front of people who are starving.

1211
00:50:52,599 --> 00:50:55,001
Ah, so that's the point.

1212
00:50:55,301 --> 00:50:57,102
Public utilities provided by the state,

1213
00:50:57,103 --> 00:50:59,805
essentials like water and power...

1214
00:50:59,806 --> 00:51:01,207
are guaranteed by the government.

1215
00:51:01,208 --> 00:51:03,809
That's why it has to come
out of the community fund.

1216
00:51:04,054 --> 00:51:07,823
Some people end up fully
dependent on state welfare.

1217
00:51:07,848 --> 00:51:10,183
- Exactly.
- And if your balance is zero,

1218
00:51:10,527 --> 00:51:12,862
they can't tax you into debt...

1219
00:51:12,887 --> 00:51:15,688
or force you into negative numbers.

1220
00:51:16,623 --> 00:51:20,826
I'm experiencing every walk of life
in this game.

1221
00:51:20,961 --> 00:51:23,128
I'm living strictly day to day.
There's no tomorrow.

1222
00:51:23,129 --> 00:51:25,497
If the state is handing out freebies,

1223
00:51:25,498 --> 00:51:26,532
I'm taking them.

1224
00:51:35,609 --> 00:51:37,476
Wait, what?

1225
00:51:37,679 --> 00:51:39,178
What's going on?

1226
00:51:40,881 --> 00:51:42,149
Did this come up earlier?

1227
00:51:43,851 --> 00:51:45,051
Fermi problems?

1228
00:51:45,052 --> 00:51:48,588
The game we just played was called
"Fermi Estimation."

1229
00:51:48,589 --> 00:51:51,491
- That was the title of the game.
- Really?

1230
00:51:51,701 --> 00:51:54,270
("Fermi Estimation,
How to Train Your Brain")

1231
00:51:54,295 --> 00:51:55,261
Jackpot!

1232
00:51:55,628 --> 00:51:58,230
One of these could easily
be the next mission.

1233
00:51:58,799 --> 00:51:59,999
We need to share this with everyone.

1234
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:02,335
- Absolutely.
- This is huge.

1235
00:52:02,470 --> 00:52:04,826
Tomorrow's challenge might
come straight from here.

1236
00:52:04,850 --> 00:52:05,705
It definitely could.

1237
00:52:05,706 --> 00:52:07,941
Game theory might actually come into play.

1238
00:52:07,942 --> 00:52:09,242
Game theory.

1239
00:52:09,243 --> 00:52:11,577
These are poets.

1240
00:52:11,578 --> 00:52:13,079
Right, classic Korean authors.

1241
00:52:13,080 --> 00:52:14,680
What would they even test us on
with literature?

1242
00:52:14,681 --> 00:52:16,582
How do you turn this into trivia?

1243
00:52:17,351 --> 00:52:18,318
That's brutal.

1244
00:52:19,453 --> 00:52:21,855
Yeah, you can't just cram that
in one night.

1245
00:52:21,856 --> 00:52:22,855
Exactly.

1246
00:52:23,423 --> 00:52:24,658
Wait, what's taking so long?

1247
00:52:26,160 --> 00:52:27,660
Where's the food?

1248
00:52:28,295 --> 00:52:29,228
Fried chicken!

1249
00:52:34,034 --> 00:52:36,135
Can I talk to you for a second?

1250
00:52:37,271 --> 00:52:38,413
Just between the two of us.

1251
00:52:38,438 --> 00:52:39,605
Sure.

1252
00:52:41,175 --> 00:52:43,076
Want to step outside?

1253
00:52:43,077 --> 00:52:44,577
Yeah, let's walk.

1254
00:52:45,746 --> 00:52:47,947
- Which room is yours?
- Upstairs, down that way.

1255
00:52:48,616 --> 00:52:50,749
- The last door on the end.
- Got it.

1256
00:52:55,623 --> 00:52:57,690
- I really trust you.
- Thanks.

1257
00:52:58,192 --> 00:52:59,292
There's something I thought
you should know.

1258
00:52:59,293 --> 00:53:01,694
I could tell the others,

1259
00:53:01,695 --> 00:53:03,429
but honestly,

1260
00:53:03,430 --> 00:53:06,432
a lot of intel was floating around
while you guys were away.

1261
00:53:06,433 --> 00:53:10,370
The kind of info that divides people...

1262
00:53:10,605 --> 00:53:12,605
and creates smaller cliques.

1263
00:53:13,540 --> 00:53:15,708
Here's what went down.

1264
00:53:16,844 --> 00:53:20,313
The second people start
withholding information...

1265
00:53:20,314 --> 00:53:23,325
that should be shared
with the whole house,

1266
00:53:23,350 --> 00:53:24,919
factions start forming.

1267
00:53:24,920 --> 00:53:27,120
That's how rifts begin.

1268
00:53:27,688 --> 00:53:29,923
I just wanted to make sure
she was aware...

1269
00:53:29,948 --> 00:53:32,158
that this kind of talk was even happening.

1270
00:53:32,159 --> 00:53:34,661
So you're giving me a heads-up...

1271
00:53:34,662 --> 00:53:36,298
- on where things stand.
- Exactly.

1272
00:53:36,299 --> 00:53:39,965
That Gray got singled out as the spy
while we were gone,

1273
00:53:39,966 --> 00:53:42,302
and Michael went around
lobbying everyone...

1274
00:53:42,303 --> 00:53:43,803
to vote him in as leader.

1275
00:53:43,804 --> 00:53:45,539
You wanted me to know about that.

1276
00:53:45,540 --> 00:53:46,439
Yes.

1277
00:53:53,914 --> 00:53:55,314
Is there...

1278
00:53:55,315 --> 00:53:58,951
something specific you need from me?

1279
00:53:59,152 --> 00:54:00,821
Well, please keep me in the game.

1280
00:54:02,890 --> 00:54:04,691
It feels like I'm the only one on edge.

1281
00:54:04,692 --> 00:54:06,493
- My score is an 11.
- Right.

1282
00:54:06,494 --> 00:54:08,628
The higher your score,

1283
00:54:08,629 --> 00:54:10,663
the more obvious
your political leaning becomes.

1284
00:54:10,664 --> 00:54:13,100
- Exactly.
- Which makes you an easy target.

1285
00:54:13,101 --> 00:54:15,134
So I'm doing everything I can to survive.

1286
00:54:15,135 --> 00:54:16,269
My score is pretty high too.

1287
00:54:16,270 --> 00:54:18,471
We're both sitting targets.

1288
00:54:18,472 --> 00:54:20,740
- That's why I'm reaching out.
- Understood.

1289
00:54:21,074 --> 00:54:23,542
Eliminations kick off today,

1290
00:54:23,543 --> 00:54:26,679
so every single round matters.

1291
00:54:26,680 --> 00:54:28,949
Going around broadcasting
this kind of dirt...

1292
00:54:29,016 --> 00:54:32,251
might actually put
a bigger target on your back.

1293
00:54:32,252 --> 00:54:34,888
I think the real reason
she pulled me aside...

1294
00:54:35,188 --> 00:54:37,422
was to fish for my immunity shield.

1295
00:54:37,423 --> 00:54:39,958
That was the subtext.

1296
00:54:40,227 --> 00:54:43,729
I couldn't promise it to her,

1297
00:54:43,730 --> 00:54:45,897
but flat-out refusing felt too harsh,

1298
00:54:45,898 --> 00:54:48,935
so I just smiled and kept quiet.

1299
00:54:48,936 --> 00:54:52,104
I thanked her for the heads-up
and wrapped up the chat.

1300
00:54:52,406 --> 00:54:54,940
Delivery has arrived.

1301
00:54:57,210 --> 00:54:58,443
Smells incredible!

1302
00:54:58,645 --> 00:54:59,880
That smells amazing!

1303
00:55:00,247 --> 00:55:01,547
Let's eat!

1304
00:55:01,716 --> 00:55:02,748
Dig in, everyone!

1305
00:55:02,749 --> 00:55:04,683
- Good work today, guys. Enjoy.
- Thanks.

1306
00:55:04,684 --> 00:55:06,185
This is unbelievable.

1307
00:55:06,787 --> 00:55:08,655
Meat instantly cures everything.

1308
00:55:11,324 --> 00:55:12,325
So good.

1309
00:55:13,293 --> 00:55:15,227
Are you a math guy?

1310
00:55:15,729 --> 00:55:16,595
Numbers?

1311
00:55:16,798 --> 00:55:19,366
I deal with data and stats
all day at work.

1312
00:55:19,900 --> 00:55:22,602
I was asking the challenge team
about the mission,

1313
00:55:22,803 --> 00:55:24,003
and they couldn't stop raving about you.

1314
00:55:24,004 --> 00:55:25,037
I heard.

1315
00:55:25,473 --> 00:55:26,872
They were giving Benjamin all the credit.

1316
00:55:26,873 --> 00:55:28,842
I barely did anything, honestly.

1317
00:55:30,044 --> 00:55:31,044
Are you always this modest?

1318
00:55:31,045 --> 00:55:34,313
I really didn't feel
like I contributed that much.

1319
00:55:36,783 --> 00:55:37,950
It was so surreal.

1320
00:55:38,185 --> 00:55:40,686
Why would they say that?

1321
00:55:41,155 --> 00:55:42,622
I honestly didn't get it.

1322
00:55:42,623 --> 00:55:45,291
I barely spoke a word.
I was just doing multiplication on paper.

1323
00:55:45,292 --> 00:55:48,661
I genuinely don't understand
why they're praising me so much.

1324
00:55:48,961 --> 00:55:51,230
Works in my favor, though.

1325
00:55:51,231 --> 00:55:52,965
Nobody sees me as a threat.

1326
00:55:53,366 --> 00:55:55,167
They chose me...

1327
00:55:55,168 --> 00:55:57,069
to go into the field with them.

1328
00:55:57,070 --> 00:55:58,202
I did the bare minimum,

1329
00:55:58,226 --> 00:56:00,140
yet they clapped
and took my word for everything.

1330
00:56:00,340 --> 00:56:02,241
They're genuinely trusting people.

1331
00:56:04,211 --> 00:56:06,746
Hey spy, stop pigging out on our budget.

1332
00:56:10,083 --> 00:56:12,051
(Official announcement begins.)

1333
00:56:17,290 --> 00:56:20,158
Attention, residents.
An announcement from the Community Center.

1334
00:56:20,660 --> 00:56:23,328
Regarding tonight's post-dinner schedule,

1335
00:56:23,497 --> 00:56:27,600
we will now disclose profile survey data
from all residents.

1336
00:56:27,601 --> 00:56:28,768
Tonight's schedule already?

1337
00:56:28,869 --> 00:56:29,869
We're still eating.

1338
00:56:30,370 --> 00:56:33,105
Here are the aggregated responses...

1339
00:56:33,106 --> 00:56:35,107
to the following survey prompt.

1340
00:56:39,446 --> 00:56:42,681
"Which Korean president
do you respect the most?"

1341
00:56:42,682 --> 00:56:44,984
(Which Korean president
do you respect the most?)

1342
00:56:51,191 --> 00:56:53,759
Park Chung Hee, two votes.

1343
00:56:54,261 --> 00:56:56,996
Roh Tae Woo, one vote.

1344
00:56:57,330 --> 00:56:59,965
Kim Dae Jung, two votes.

1345
00:57:00,434 --> 00:57:03,302
Roh Moo Hyun, three votes.

1346
00:57:04,071 --> 00:57:05,872
None, four votes.

1347
00:57:12,579 --> 00:57:13,546
Interesting.

1348
00:57:13,547 --> 00:57:15,281
- That's wild.
- Fascinating spread.

1349
00:57:15,649 --> 00:57:16,515
Quite a mix.

1350
00:57:16,516 --> 00:57:17,882
Tonight's theme is politics.

1351
00:57:17,883 --> 00:57:18,984
Yep, political ideology.

1352
00:57:18,985 --> 00:57:20,686
We're getting into heavy politics tonight.

1353
00:57:20,954 --> 00:57:22,722
What's the biggest surprise to you?

1354
00:57:23,257 --> 00:57:24,457
Most surprising?

1355
00:57:24,591 --> 00:57:25,457
I'd say...

1356
00:57:25,659 --> 00:57:27,126
four people picking "None."

1357
00:57:29,696 --> 00:57:31,029
Which presidents were completely left out?

1358
00:57:31,030 --> 00:57:33,166
Kim Young Sam didn't get a mention.

1359
00:57:33,700 --> 00:57:35,901
- Lee Myung Bak.
- Lee Myung Bak, neither.

1360
00:57:35,902 --> 00:57:37,536
- Park Geun Hye.
- Park Geun Hye either.

1361
00:57:37,561 --> 00:57:39,362
Well, Park Geun Hye...

1362
00:57:39,739 --> 00:57:41,241
is still too controversial to evaluate.

1363
00:57:41,508 --> 00:57:44,443
Moon Jae In didn't show up either.

1364
00:57:44,444 --> 00:57:45,878
- You're right!
- True!

1365
00:57:45,879 --> 00:57:47,147
He's not in for the vote.

1366
00:57:47,148 --> 00:57:49,748
- That's unexpected.
- Moon Jae In.

1367
00:57:50,617 --> 00:57:54,353
Maybe people deliberately chose presidents
who have passed away.

1368
00:57:54,354 --> 00:57:56,956
Figures you can evaluate
with historical hindsight.

1369
00:57:57,457 --> 00:57:58,724
That's a fair point.

1370
00:58:01,328 --> 00:58:04,964
The conservative and progressive picks...

1371
00:58:04,965 --> 00:58:06,866
are almost perfectly balanced.

1372
00:58:06,867 --> 00:58:09,235
Looks like our house is split
right down the middle politically.

1373
00:58:09,236 --> 00:58:11,103
Quite an eclectic mix.

1374
00:58:11,104 --> 00:58:15,408
What caught my eye was
Roh Moo Hyun leading the count.

1375
00:58:15,409 --> 00:58:18,044
Park Chung Hee was expected.

1376
00:58:18,045 --> 00:58:20,713
Roh Tae Woo getting a vote
really caught me off guard, though.

1377
00:58:20,714 --> 00:58:22,047
What's the story there?

1378
00:58:22,048 --> 00:58:25,952
What really grabbed me was
the four "None" votes.

1379
00:58:25,953 --> 00:58:28,955
Maybe they chose "None"...

1380
00:58:29,789 --> 00:58:32,724
because they're completely
disillusioned with the whole system.

1381
00:58:32,725 --> 00:58:35,260
To be blunt, I couldn't
care less about politics.

1382
00:58:35,762 --> 00:58:37,196
Whoever holds the presidency,

1383
00:58:37,197 --> 00:58:40,242
whether they run the country well
or run it into the ground,

1384
00:58:40,267 --> 00:58:42,167
it doesn't put ten bucks in my pocket.

1385
00:58:42,168 --> 00:58:45,137
No matter who's in office,
nothing trickles down to me.

1386
00:58:45,305 --> 00:58:49,009
I don't even revere figures
like King Sejong or Admiral Yi Sun Sin.

1387
00:58:49,476 --> 00:58:52,379
When King Sejong codified Hangul,

1388
00:58:52,779 --> 00:58:55,147
or when Admiral Yi built the turtle ships,

1389
00:58:55,498 --> 00:58:57,784
did they break their backs
building them by hand?

1390
00:58:58,018 --> 00:58:59,752
It was the nameless subordinates
who suffered.

1391
00:58:59,753 --> 00:59:01,487
Leadership just signed the decrees.

1392
00:59:02,322 --> 00:59:03,455
Let's be real.

1393
00:59:03,657 --> 00:59:06,025
Why does everyone else
get erased from history?

1394
00:59:06,026 --> 00:59:08,094
Sure, maybe a policy shift
helps me out on paper.

1395
00:59:08,095 --> 00:59:10,997
Maybe high school becomes mandatory
and state-funded.

1396
00:59:10,998 --> 00:59:13,600
"I couldn't afford tuition for my kid,"

1397
00:59:13,601 --> 00:59:15,402
"and the government came through
with a subsidy."

1398
00:59:15,503 --> 00:59:17,437
If that's what makes you grateful,

1399
00:59:17,704 --> 00:59:19,954
it's because you're living
at the bottom of the ladder.

1400
00:59:19,978 --> 00:59:21,545
So I want these people...

1401
00:59:21,546 --> 00:59:24,147
to become people
who don't matter to me at all,

1402
00:59:24,148 --> 00:59:26,817
regardless of what they do.

1403
00:59:27,686 --> 00:59:30,387
The anonymous chat is starting soon.

1404
00:59:31,022 --> 00:59:32,990
Please stand by.

1405
00:59:33,897 --> 00:59:35,292
Did you eat enough?

1406
00:59:35,293 --> 00:59:36,427
- Did you eat a lot?
- I did.

1407
00:59:36,428 --> 00:59:38,896
- Did you too?
- I did. I'm so full right now.

1408
00:59:38,897 --> 00:59:40,998
We need to stay sharp from now on.

1409
00:59:41,933 --> 00:59:45,903
Applications for today's debate
are now open.

1410
00:59:45,904 --> 00:59:48,305
Today's topic is politics.

1411
00:59:48,306 --> 00:59:49,740
I want to try.

1412
00:59:51,776 --> 00:59:54,644
The debate topic is as follows:

1413
00:59:55,386 --> 00:59:57,087
"There are times..."

1414
00:59:57,615 --> 01:00:01,451
"when a country needs
a competent dictator for its development."

1415
01:00:07,725 --> 01:00:12,929
(Username: hikkan16)

1416
01:00:12,930 --> 01:00:16,433
(Gray used the username he uses
in real life.)

1417
01:00:16,434 --> 01:00:18,635
"A country needs
a competent dictator for its development."

1418
01:00:19,471 --> 01:00:20,671
"A competent dictator."

1419
01:00:22,674 --> 01:00:23,974
(Against: Gray)

1420
01:00:23,975 --> 01:00:24,975
Yes!

1421
01:00:29,013 --> 01:00:32,615
My OIKOS username is "kebabmania82."

1422
01:00:33,117 --> 01:00:35,318
Kebab just came to mind.

1423
01:00:35,853 --> 01:00:37,754
I thought it might sound like it's a guy.

1424
01:00:37,755 --> 01:00:40,857
(Against: Gray, Hippo)

1425
01:00:42,494 --> 01:00:45,162
"Luminous" is one
of the English words I like.

1426
01:00:45,163 --> 01:00:48,765
I chose a username that means...

1427
01:00:48,766 --> 01:00:50,133
"shining in the darkness."

1428
01:00:50,735 --> 01:00:51,668
I found it.

1429
01:00:51,669 --> 01:00:53,770
(Against: Gray, Hippo, Polar Bear)

1430
01:00:54,172 --> 01:00:56,240
My username is "hahahak."

1431
01:00:56,474 --> 01:00:59,843
I just put "hahaha"
and added a K at the end...

1432
01:00:59,844 --> 01:01:01,778
because there's no K in my name.

1433
01:01:02,180 --> 01:01:05,215
I put a picture of a woman
as my profile picture.

1434
01:01:05,650 --> 01:01:06,750
A woman from behind.

1435
01:01:07,119 --> 01:01:09,820
A woman's silhouette
that's hard to tell from a man's.

1436
01:01:10,154 --> 01:01:13,923
I wanted my username to sound
like a woman's...

1437
01:01:14,359 --> 01:01:16,126
so people wouldn't suspect it's me.

1438
01:01:17,628 --> 01:01:18,862
My username is...

1439
01:01:19,330 --> 01:01:20,697
"blackpink."

1440
01:01:22,733 --> 01:01:25,869
I wanted a username
that had nothing to do with me,

1441
01:01:26,337 --> 01:01:28,805
and I ended up choosing "blackpink."

1442
01:01:29,674 --> 01:01:30,907
No other reason.

1443
01:01:30,908 --> 01:01:31,975
(For: Dark Night, Superman)

1444
01:01:32,277 --> 01:01:35,913
If I'm confident about the topic,
I should participate.

1445
01:01:35,914 --> 01:01:37,514
(Nangja)

1446
01:01:38,116 --> 01:01:40,918
I have to join today.

1447
01:01:41,753 --> 01:01:42,786
Goodness.

1448
01:01:43,030 --> 01:01:45,115
(For: Dark Night, Superman, Nangja)

1449
01:01:45,140 --> 01:01:47,691
("There are times when a country needs
a competent dictator for development.")

1450
01:01:47,692 --> 01:01:50,652
(Against: Gray, Polar Bear, Hippo)

1451
01:01:50,653 --> 01:01:53,597
(For: Nangja, Dark Night, Superman)

1452
01:01:53,999 --> 01:01:56,166
The chat will now begin.

1453
01:01:56,834 --> 01:01:58,668
Please remove your blindfolds.

1454
01:02:04,041 --> 01:02:06,409
(For: Superman, Dark Night, Nangja)

1455
01:02:06,410 --> 01:02:11,381
(Against: Gray, Hippo, Polar Bear)

1456
01:02:11,382 --> 01:02:13,683
(The debate begins.)

1457
01:02:14,852 --> 01:02:16,186
(Since it says a dictator is necessary,)

1458
01:02:16,187 --> 01:02:18,455
(I want to hear the reasons first.)

1459
01:02:18,456 --> 01:02:20,323
(The phrase "a competent dictator"
stands out to me.)

1460
01:02:20,324 --> 01:02:23,493
(Let's define that first
and move on from there.)

1461
01:02:24,095 --> 01:02:26,196
(We also need to focus on the phrase
"there are times.")

1462
01:02:26,197 --> 01:02:29,366
(We need to define what we mean
by "national development" too.)

1463
01:02:29,634 --> 01:02:30,567
What?

1464
01:02:33,838 --> 01:02:35,438
(I'm on the For side,)

1465
01:02:35,439 --> 01:02:37,745
(but I don't think dictators
are necessary...)

1466
01:02:37,746 --> 01:02:40,043
(during every stage
of a country's development.)

1467
01:02:40,044 --> 01:02:44,514
(The stages where a dictator is necessary
are very limited.)

1468
01:02:44,515 --> 01:02:47,150
(At what stage would you actually agree
that a dictator is necessary?)

1469
01:02:47,385 --> 01:02:49,853
(A dictatorship is more likely to work...)

1470
01:02:49,854 --> 01:02:55,392
(in a developing country
than in a developed country.)

1471
01:02:55,927 --> 01:02:56,527
(Think about Korea.)

1472
01:02:56,528 --> 01:02:57,661
(I want to bring up the period...)

1473
01:02:57,662 --> 01:02:59,463
(when democracy was put aside
in favor of economic development.)

1474
01:02:59,464 --> 01:03:02,366
(The Saemaul Movement and the construction
of major highways. Most people know...)

1475
01:03:02,367 --> 01:03:06,537
(about our rapid economic growth
under Park Chung Hee.)

1476
01:03:06,538 --> 01:03:09,072
(I was talking about the same period
as hahahak.)

1477
01:03:09,340 --> 01:03:12,609
(Most people would agree...)

1478
01:03:12,610 --> 01:03:16,246
(that Park Chung Hee's government was
a dictatorship.)

1479
01:03:16,247 --> 01:03:19,549
(I think most people had
that same period in mind...)

1480
01:03:19,550 --> 01:03:22,852
(when they picked a side.)

1481
01:03:25,022 --> 01:03:27,056
Actually, today's debate starts...

1482
01:03:27,057 --> 01:03:29,926
with how we define...

1483
01:03:29,927 --> 01:03:31,427
the Park Chung Hee administration.

1484
01:03:31,428 --> 01:03:32,896
Because of that,

1485
01:03:32,897 --> 01:03:35,865
people with conservative views
naturally joined in.

1486
01:03:36,677 --> 01:03:37,960
(Right, equalism,
upper-middle, conservative)

1487
01:03:37,985 --> 01:03:39,376
(Right, equalism,
working class, conservative)

1488
01:03:39,401 --> 01:03:41,305
(Right, equalism,
working class, conservative)

1489
01:03:41,306 --> 01:03:42,534
(Left, feminism,
working class, open-minded)

1490
01:03:42,559 --> 01:03:44,129
(Left, feminism,
upper-middle, open-minded)

1491
01:03:44,153 --> 01:03:47,411
(Left, feminism,
working class, open-minded)

1492
01:03:47,745 --> 01:03:49,813
(I agree that there were
economic achievements during that period.)

1493
01:03:49,814 --> 01:03:54,317
(We should challenge the idea...)

1494
01:03:54,318 --> 01:03:58,088
(that Korea's economic success
was achieved...)

1495
01:03:58,089 --> 01:04:01,725
(because of the dictator, Park Chung Hee.)

1496
01:04:02,460 --> 01:04:05,162
(A lot of people probably think...)

1497
01:04:05,163 --> 01:04:07,230
(that the economic development plans
at that time were...)

1498
01:04:07,231 --> 01:04:10,000
(the product of Park Chung Hee's
dictatorship.)

1499
01:04:10,001 --> 01:04:12,436
(But there's another argument...)

1500
01:04:12,437 --> 01:04:15,005
(that it didn't suddenly appear
under Park Chung Hee.)

1501
01:04:18,242 --> 01:04:22,946
(The idea of establishing
an economic development plan was...)

1502
01:04:22,947 --> 01:04:26,183
(already included
in the provisional constitution in 1919.)

1503
01:04:26,184 --> 01:04:28,652
(It's a bit of a stretch to say...)

1504
01:04:28,653 --> 01:04:31,955
(that he, as an individual dictator,
was solely responsible...)

1505
01:04:31,980 --> 01:04:34,548
(for Korea's economic development.)

1506
01:04:35,026 --> 01:04:37,194
(But that's just an opinion.)

1507
01:04:37,195 --> 01:04:39,162
(I agree with luminous1101.)

1508
01:04:39,163 --> 01:04:42,799
(There were also countless people
who quietly worked behind the scenes.)

1509
01:04:43,367 --> 01:04:46,233
(I tend to think of leaders more...)

1510
01:04:46,234 --> 01:04:49,038
(as symbols or products of their time.)

1511
01:04:49,039 --> 01:04:53,643
(The circumstances have to be there
for a certain leader to emerge...)

1512
01:04:53,644 --> 01:04:55,778
(and then that person plays their role.)

1513
01:04:55,779 --> 01:04:57,580
Do they?

1514
01:04:58,158 --> 01:05:02,495
(Even if Park Chung Hee hadn't existed,
someone similar might have come along...)

1515
01:05:02,520 --> 01:05:08,124
(as long as the circumstances
of the time were right.)

1516
01:05:08,125 --> 01:05:11,194
(I don't really agree with that.)

1517
01:05:11,195 --> 01:05:15,331
(He made a lot of political decisions...)

1518
01:05:15,332 --> 01:05:18,702
(that can't just be explained
by the circumstances of the time.)

1519
01:05:18,703 --> 01:05:19,836
(I disagree with them too.)

1520
01:05:19,837 --> 01:05:21,137
(It was possible because it was him.)

1521
01:05:21,138 --> 01:05:22,731
(Just like Hangul was created
because of King Sejong...)

1522
01:05:22,756 --> 01:05:26,009
(and the turtle ships were developed
because of Admiral Yi Sun Sin.)

1523
01:05:26,310 --> 01:05:28,812
(Without him, Korea wouldn't be
the same country it is today.)

1524
01:05:28,813 --> 01:05:30,647
(No one could've replaced him.)

1525
01:05:30,648 --> 01:05:34,784
(Name one person
who could've replaced him.)

1526
01:05:36,029 --> 01:05:39,732
(Countless people had their rights
and freedoms taken away,)

1527
01:05:39,757 --> 01:05:43,026
(and a lot of innocent people lost
their lives.)

1528
01:05:43,027 --> 01:05:46,625
(Can all of that simply be erased
because there were economic achievements?)

1529
01:05:48,599 --> 01:05:53,202
(If Korea reaches another point
when we need a competent dictator,)

1530
01:05:53,203 --> 01:05:57,206
(and that dictator repeats
what happened in the past...)

1531
01:05:57,207 --> 01:06:01,077
(in the name of economic growth,
would you still support it?)

1532
01:06:01,445 --> 01:06:04,653
(Can we really define
national development...)

1533
01:06:04,654 --> 01:06:08,017
(by economic growth alone?)

1534
01:06:08,018 --> 01:06:09,852
(Sacrifice of people and economic growth.)

1535
01:06:09,853 --> 01:06:11,554
They shouldn't see...

1536
01:06:11,822 --> 01:06:13,456
national development that way.

1537
01:06:13,457 --> 01:06:16,793
(Can you really say
one is more important than the other?)

1538
01:06:17,361 --> 01:06:21,130
(You can't give us two choices and say
choosing one means it's more valuable.)

1539
01:06:21,131 --> 01:06:24,400
(Did those people choose to sacrifice
themselves, or were they forced into it?)

1540
01:06:24,969 --> 01:06:27,771
(I'm not talking about the people
who sacrificed themselves voluntarily.)

1541
01:06:27,772 --> 01:06:31,078
(I'm talking about innocent people
who lost their lives under that regime.)

1542
01:06:33,377 --> 01:06:36,079
(The topic is "national development.")

1543
01:06:36,080 --> 01:06:38,915
(Focus on whether we need it or not.)

1544
01:06:38,916 --> 01:06:42,452
(Nobody here is saying
that human life isn't valuable.)

1545
01:06:42,453 --> 01:06:45,322
(Why are you turning it
into an either-or question?)

1546
01:06:46,290 --> 01:06:50,827
(Just because there were economic
achievements under Park Chung Hee,)

1547
01:06:50,828 --> 01:06:54,157
(we can't call
the sacrifice of countless lives...)

1548
01:06:54,158 --> 01:06:57,534
("national development," can we?)

1549
01:06:57,535 --> 01:07:00,904
(I think it can still be considered
national development.)

1550
01:07:00,905 --> 01:07:04,841
("Innocent people were sacrificed
during a country's development.)

1551
01:07:04,842 --> 01:07:09,412
(You can't deny that the country developed
just because some people are unhappy.)

1552
01:07:09,814 --> 01:07:10,981
(It's like someone winning
a gold medal...)

1553
01:07:10,982 --> 01:07:12,316
(and another person saying,)

1554
01:07:12,317 --> 01:07:14,151
("I don't recognize that gold medal...")

1555
01:07:14,152 --> 01:07:16,786
("because I don't like him
and he hurt me.")

1556
01:07:16,818 --> 01:07:19,187
(That's how it sounds to me.)

1557
01:07:22,393 --> 01:07:24,227
I think national development...

1558
01:07:24,228 --> 01:07:26,062
has to be evaluated...

1559
01:07:26,063 --> 01:07:28,031
both quantitatively and qualitatively.

1560
01:07:28,299 --> 01:07:29,666
If the economy grows...

1561
01:07:29,667 --> 01:07:32,235
but innocent people are killed...

1562
01:07:32,236 --> 01:07:33,603
and the media is suppressed,

1563
01:07:33,705 --> 01:07:36,739
you can't just say, "The economy grew,
so I guess it was worth it."

1564
01:07:36,740 --> 01:07:39,609
That wouldn't be a good country.

1565
01:07:39,876 --> 01:07:42,846
The country grew,
and the economy developed.

1566
01:07:42,847 --> 01:07:46,049
But many people refuse
to recognize that...

1567
01:07:46,050 --> 01:07:48,618
because innocent people were sacrificed.

1568
01:07:48,619 --> 01:07:50,419
So what?

1569
01:07:50,420 --> 01:07:52,221
So what do you want to do about it?

1570
01:07:52,957 --> 01:07:55,358
(Some people say...)

1571
01:07:55,359 --> 01:07:58,361
(Trump's idea of making
America great again...)

1572
01:07:58,362 --> 01:07:59,829
(and his conservative movement...)

1573
01:07:59,830 --> 01:08:01,097
(have authoritarian tendencies.)

1574
01:08:01,098 --> 01:08:02,632
(But a lot of moderate voters accepted
Trump...)

1575
01:08:02,633 --> 01:08:04,901
(because they wanted to get
out of an economic downturn.)

1576
01:08:05,970 --> 01:08:08,538
(I'm genuinely asking.)

1577
01:08:08,539 --> 01:08:12,409
(Did the US economy actually get better
after Trump took office?)

1578
01:08:12,509 --> 01:08:16,178
(It doesn't matter
if it got better or not.)

1579
01:08:16,179 --> 01:08:18,180
(Yes, it does.)

1580
01:08:18,181 --> 01:08:20,816
(The point is whether there are times
where a dictatorship is necessary.)

1581
01:08:20,817 --> 01:08:22,985
(If it didn't improve,
then he only used...)

1582
01:08:22,986 --> 01:08:25,688
("economic development" to persuade
people, so it's a bad example.)

1583
01:08:27,024 --> 01:08:31,160
(If you look at GDP,
national defense power,)

1584
01:08:31,161 --> 01:08:33,263
(Korea joining the OECD,)

1585
01:08:33,264 --> 01:08:36,933
(and pretty much every major measure
of national competitiveness,)

1586
01:08:36,934 --> 01:08:40,737
(Korea made a huge leap forward
during the Park Chung Hee era.)

1587
01:08:42,573 --> 01:08:45,708
(Economically, Korea clearly developed.)

1588
01:08:45,709 --> 01:08:48,878
(But did we become happier?)

1589
01:08:48,879 --> 01:08:52,248
(Innocent people lost their lives
during the process,)

1590
01:08:52,249 --> 01:08:55,718
(and Korea eventually achieved
democratization through that pain.)

1591
01:08:56,387 --> 01:08:58,421
(We can look at "development"
from a lot of different angles.)

1592
01:08:58,422 --> 01:09:00,724
(I agree with that interpretation.)

1593
01:09:00,725 --> 01:09:01,891
Even if we define national development...

1594
01:09:01,892 --> 01:09:03,906
(Even if we define national development
as people's happiness and well-being,)

1595
01:09:03,931 --> 01:09:06,529
as people's happiness and well-being...

1596
01:09:06,697 --> 01:09:11,467
(I still think we need a leader...)

1597
01:09:11,468 --> 01:09:13,036
(who is capable...)

1598
01:09:13,037 --> 01:09:17,573
(of forcing that kind of change,
even if they're called a dictator.)

1599
01:09:18,209 --> 01:09:21,482
(If we follow
the Against side's argument,)

1600
01:09:21,483 --> 01:09:24,815
(does that mean every national policy
has to go only in a direction...)

1601
01:09:24,816 --> 01:09:27,117
(that the majority of people
have fully agreed on,)

1602
01:09:27,118 --> 01:09:29,953
(even if that means moving very slowly?)

1603
01:09:29,954 --> 01:09:34,991
(Isn't public consensus
the bare minimum in a democracy?)

1604
01:09:36,660 --> 01:09:40,596
(It's also a bit of a stereotype to say
that consensus means moving slowly.)

1605
01:09:40,597 --> 01:09:43,533
(It could be even faster.)

1606
01:09:44,234 --> 01:09:46,135
(We're talking about development.
If we had enough discussion...)

1607
01:09:46,136 --> 01:09:48,175
(and still faced the IMF crisis, can we
say that it helped the country grow?)

1608
01:09:48,200 --> 01:09:50,940
(You might say it was for the people,
but did the people want that?)

1609
01:09:50,941 --> 01:09:53,843
(Does it have to be what the people want
before we call it national development?)

1610
01:09:53,844 --> 01:09:57,346
(How are you supposed
to make everyone happy?)

1611
01:09:57,347 --> 01:10:02,284
(If you have an answer to that,
let me hear it.)

1612
01:10:02,619 --> 01:10:04,987
(It's impossible
to make everyone happy...)

1613
01:10:04,988 --> 01:10:07,122
(and it's very dangerous.)

1614
01:10:07,123 --> 01:10:10,826
(That's why I'm against dictatorship
without adding any conditions.)

1615
01:10:12,730 --> 01:10:15,398
Let me check the feed.

1616
01:10:15,399 --> 01:10:17,768
(A dictator is not the same
as a competent dictator.)

1617
01:10:17,793 --> 01:10:20,894
"People's basic freedoms
aren't protected..."

1618
01:10:21,872 --> 01:10:24,340
"As long as everyone has freedom..."

1619
01:10:25,009 --> 01:10:26,843
"The country becomes poor..."

1620
01:10:30,781 --> 01:10:32,281
(I saw this in the feed.)

1621
01:10:32,282 --> 01:10:34,150
(To the For side,
as long as a country becomes richer,)

1622
01:10:34,151 --> 01:10:35,952
(is that enough even if people's
basic freedoms aren't protected?)

1623
01:10:35,953 --> 01:10:38,187
(To the Against side,
as long as everyone has freedom,)

1624
01:10:38,188 --> 01:10:40,823
(do you not mind falling
behind the rest of the world?)

1625
01:10:40,824 --> 01:10:44,394
(I saw this comment.)

1626
01:10:45,029 --> 01:10:46,029
(If I had to choose,)

1627
01:10:46,030 --> 01:10:49,666
(I'd rather live a free but poor life,
even if I fall behind.)

1628
01:10:49,667 --> 01:10:52,669
(That's your life.)

1629
01:10:52,670 --> 01:10:55,305
(Your own happiness has nothing to do
with national development.)

1630
01:10:55,330 --> 01:10:57,464
"That's your life."

1631
01:10:57,675 --> 01:11:00,210
I like him.

1632
01:11:00,577 --> 01:11:02,211
(I know that.)

1633
01:11:02,212 --> 01:11:06,715
(I'm just saying I value the latter more.)

1634
01:11:07,051 --> 01:11:10,320
(What the country gains
by taking away people's freedoms...)

1635
01:11:10,321 --> 01:11:14,324
(doesn't necessarily benefit everyone.)

1636
01:11:14,325 --> 01:11:17,260
(Freedom is a basic human right.)

1637
01:11:17,261 --> 01:11:20,094
(If a country's development makes
its own people miserable,)

1638
01:11:20,095 --> 01:11:23,266
(then isn't that just development
in name only?)

1639
01:11:23,901 --> 01:11:25,239
(Exactly. "Development in name only.")

1640
01:11:25,264 --> 01:11:28,838
(That's still national development.)

1641
01:11:28,839 --> 01:11:30,874
"In name only." That sounds so funny.

1642
01:11:31,775 --> 01:11:33,709
He's making fun of her.

1643
01:11:34,345 --> 01:11:37,314
Hahahak was so funny.

1644
01:11:37,315 --> 01:11:40,417
He mocked them a few times,
and it was hilarious.

1645
01:11:40,418 --> 01:11:42,018
I don't know who he is,

1646
01:11:42,019 --> 01:11:45,388
but he's so good at mocking people.

1647
01:11:45,789 --> 01:11:47,589
Was it hahahak?

1648
01:11:47,590 --> 01:11:49,592
He made some sarcastic comments.

1649
01:11:49,593 --> 01:11:52,528
When he made those comments...

1650
01:11:52,529 --> 01:11:54,064
to annoy the other side,

1651
01:11:54,065 --> 01:11:56,065
I didn't really like that.

1652
01:11:57,134 --> 01:11:59,671
(Ten minutes left until the chat ends.)

1653
01:11:59,672 --> 01:12:02,571
Ten minutes left until the chat ends.

1654
01:12:02,706 --> 01:12:07,243
("There are times when a country needs
a competent dictator for development.")

1655
01:12:07,244 --> 01:12:08,660
(You can't expect people
to have a sense of morality...)

1656
01:12:08,685 --> 01:12:10,017
(without a certain level
of economic security.)

1657
01:12:10,018 --> 01:12:12,035
(Without material stability, it's hard
to expect people to prioritize morality.)

1658
01:12:12,060 --> 01:12:16,419
(If people are living in extreme poverty
and their basic survival is at stake,)

1659
01:12:16,420 --> 01:12:19,755
(some short-term damage
to democracy can be tolerated.)

1660
01:12:20,491 --> 01:12:22,625
(So for the Against side,)

1661
01:12:22,626 --> 01:12:26,529
(if people's basic freedoms are protected,
but they're economically poor,)

1662
01:12:26,530 --> 01:12:30,333
(are you okay with getting pushed
out of the global market?)

1663
01:12:30,334 --> 01:12:34,838
(Let's define national development
as moving toward a better state...)

1664
01:12:34,839 --> 01:12:38,741
(and providing people with a better life.)

1665
01:12:38,742 --> 01:12:43,313
(I think it's a huge bias to assume...)

1666
01:12:43,314 --> 01:12:46,373
(that being poor and falling behind
economically is automatically worse...)

1667
01:12:46,374 --> 01:12:49,452
(than having less freedom but living
an economically comfortable life.)

1668
01:12:52,122 --> 01:12:54,757
(The point of this debate is
national development.)

1669
01:12:54,758 --> 01:12:57,860
(We're not talking
about how happy people feel.)

1670
01:12:58,128 --> 01:13:02,264
(The Against side keeps bringing up
the right to pursue happiness.)

1671
01:13:02,265 --> 01:13:05,301
(Let's debate about that next time.)

1672
01:13:05,302 --> 01:13:08,704
(Let's focus on national development now.)

1673
01:13:09,039 --> 01:13:11,874
(What exactly makes up a country?)

1674
01:13:11,875 --> 01:13:16,679
(National development can't be reduced
to economic growth alone.)

1675
01:13:16,680 --> 01:13:19,482
(When we evaluate
national competitiveness,)

1676
01:13:19,483 --> 01:13:22,307
(we consider GDP
or national defense power,)

1677
01:13:22,331 --> 01:13:24,287
(not how happy people are.)

1678
01:13:25,022 --> 01:13:28,958
(The reason I keep bringing up
quality of life is...)

1679
01:13:28,959 --> 01:13:32,195
(because even though Korea achieved
remarkable economic development,)

1680
01:13:32,196 --> 01:13:35,365
(it still has one of the highest suicide
rates in the world,)

1681
01:13:35,366 --> 01:13:39,569
(and problems with depression
and anxiety are getting worse.)

1682
01:13:39,954 --> 01:13:45,007
(I agree that people's well-being should
be part of national development.)

1683
01:13:45,008 --> 01:13:50,413
(So, please include
economic growth as well.)

1684
01:13:50,414 --> 01:13:54,584
(We never said
economic growth doesn't matter.)

1685
01:13:55,085 --> 01:13:58,418
(We've been saying
that you can't use economic growth...)

1686
01:13:58,419 --> 01:14:01,991
(as an excuse
for restricting basic rights,)

1687
01:14:01,992 --> 01:14:04,865
(sacrificing people's lives,)

1688
01:14:04,866 --> 01:14:07,730
(and destroying democracy
under a dictatorship.)

1689
01:14:10,400 --> 01:14:14,203
(I think the opposing side's
main argument is basically this.)

1690
01:14:14,204 --> 01:14:16,376
("Democracy and human rights matter too.")

1691
01:14:16,377 --> 01:14:19,041
("Does economic development
justify dictatorship?")

1692
01:14:19,042 --> 01:14:23,279
(You found the point where we disagree.)

1693
01:14:23,513 --> 01:14:26,682
(I would say it doesn't justify it,)

1694
01:14:26,683 --> 01:14:29,284
(but even so,
I agree with the statement...)

1695
01:14:29,285 --> 01:14:33,555
(as long as the leader is competent
and the rule is short-term.)

1696
01:14:35,959 --> 01:14:38,894
It's different from the debate last night.

1697
01:14:38,996 --> 01:14:41,030
Yesterday, it felt like both sides
were just going in parallel.

1698
01:14:41,031 --> 01:14:43,165
Even when we tried to pause,

1699
01:14:43,166 --> 01:14:44,967
it didn't really work.

1700
01:14:44,968 --> 01:14:48,905
This time, both sides realized
they weren't going to convince each other,

1701
01:14:48,906 --> 01:14:52,241
and they made a real effort
to ask each other meaningful questions.

1702
01:14:53,377 --> 01:14:55,078
(I'm saying it's dangerous...)

1703
01:14:55,079 --> 01:14:59,173
(for society to think
that a dictatorship can be justified...)

1704
01:14:59,198 --> 01:15:03,477
(under certain circumstances.)

1705
01:15:07,324 --> 01:15:10,993
(A lot of dictators
probably started out thinking...)

1706
01:15:10,994 --> 01:15:15,398
(they were competent,
and that it was going to be temporary.)

1707
01:15:15,399 --> 01:15:17,633
(Park Chung Hee is a good example.)

1708
01:15:17,634 --> 01:15:22,004
(After achieving economic growth,
he focused on extending his own rule.)

1709
01:15:22,606 --> 01:15:25,441
(Honestly, I don't like dictators either.)

1710
01:15:25,442 --> 01:15:28,244
(But just because I don't like them
doesn't mean it's not necessary.)

1711
01:15:28,245 --> 01:15:31,336
(If a dictator contributed
to national development,)

1712
01:15:31,360 --> 01:15:33,216
(I would recognize that.)

1713
01:15:33,217 --> 01:15:35,952
(There's this person at work.)

1714
01:15:35,953 --> 01:15:38,087
(He treats the people under him badly,)

1715
01:15:38,088 --> 01:15:40,523
(but he's competent at his job
and contributes a lot to the company.)

1716
01:15:40,524 --> 01:15:44,494
(The people under him don't like him,
but what can they do?)

1717
01:15:44,495 --> 01:15:47,033
(Since he's good at his job,)

1718
01:15:47,034 --> 01:15:49,632
(I can still acknowledge his competence.)

1719
01:15:49,633 --> 01:15:51,634
Hahahak...

1720
01:15:51,635 --> 01:15:53,936
gave us an interesting example.

1721
01:15:53,937 --> 01:15:57,373
That's why I watched the debate
with a smile.

1722
01:15:57,374 --> 01:16:00,543
I didn't have to throw in big words...

1723
01:16:00,544 --> 01:16:03,479
or try to sound intelligent.

1724
01:16:03,714 --> 01:16:07,850
I was just sharing my thoughts and views.

1725
01:16:07,985 --> 01:16:11,020
Both sides were convincing
at different points.

1726
01:16:11,021 --> 01:16:13,823
It was really fun to watch.
I completely lost track of time.

1727
01:16:15,225 --> 01:16:17,126
The chat has ended.

1728
01:16:17,127 --> 01:16:19,461
(The chat has ended.)

1729
01:16:23,333 --> 01:16:26,502
(Voting begins.)

1730
01:16:26,970 --> 01:16:27,903
Done!

1731
01:16:27,904 --> 01:16:29,038
(Michael votes for Against.)

1732
01:16:29,039 --> 01:16:30,039
Please.

1733
01:16:30,674 --> 01:16:35,144
Their arguments were
pretty convincing to me.

1734
01:16:35,487 --> 01:16:38,181
I was pretty sure the MVP would be
from the Against side and that they'd win,

1735
01:16:38,182 --> 01:16:40,016
so I went with them.

1736
01:16:40,017 --> 01:16:41,250
(Benjamin votes for For.)

1737
01:16:41,251 --> 01:16:42,585
(For: There are times when we need
a dictator.)

1738
01:16:42,586 --> 01:16:45,288
I can understand
why people get so passionate...

1739
01:16:45,289 --> 01:16:46,121
about this topic.

1740
01:16:46,122 --> 01:16:49,492
There aren't many countries
that have developed...

1741
01:16:49,493 --> 01:16:51,027
as quickly as Korea has.

1742
01:16:51,028 --> 01:16:52,095
So I understand...

1743
01:16:52,096 --> 01:16:55,698
why some people believe
that a dictatorship was necessary.

1744
01:16:55,699 --> 01:16:57,211
(Ted votes for Against.)

1745
01:16:57,236 --> 01:16:58,934
(Against: There is no time
when a dictator is necessary.)

1746
01:16:58,935 --> 01:17:00,804
What I loved about today's debate was...

1747
01:17:00,805 --> 01:17:03,206
that both sides were able to understand
their differences...

1748
01:17:03,207 --> 01:17:07,042
and even the audience could also see
and understand...

1749
01:17:07,043 --> 01:17:08,778
why they had such different views.

1750
01:17:08,779 --> 01:17:10,308
(Genie votes for For.)

1751
01:17:12,050 --> 01:17:15,551
Depending on how you define the situation,

1752
01:17:15,552 --> 01:17:18,053
what you mean by dictatorship,

1753
01:17:18,556 --> 01:17:21,257
and what you mean by development,

1754
01:17:21,258 --> 01:17:25,294
both sides had valid arguments
on different points.

1755
01:17:25,964 --> 01:17:27,430
The vote ended...

1756
01:17:28,231 --> 01:17:30,098
in a 6 to 6 tie,

1757
01:17:30,701 --> 01:17:35,104
so the For side, which has the MVP,
has won the debate.

1758
01:17:35,105 --> 01:17:36,372
That's so funny.

1759
01:17:38,075 --> 01:17:40,042
The debater selected as MVP is...

1760
01:17:40,043 --> 01:17:41,544
What happens now?

1761
01:17:41,545 --> 01:17:43,946
totentanz.

1762
01:17:44,148 --> 01:17:46,983
Thank you.

1763
01:17:47,884 --> 01:17:50,886
Each resident on the winning side
will receive 1,000 dollars,

1764
01:17:50,887 --> 01:17:53,789
(Balance: 1,000 dollars, 1,560 dollars,
2,680 dollars)

1765
01:17:53,790 --> 01:17:57,860
while each resident on the losing side
will lose 1,000 dollars.

1766
01:17:58,128 --> 01:17:59,295
Congratulations.

1767
01:18:01,732 --> 01:18:04,434
A "competent dictator"...

1768
01:18:04,435 --> 01:18:06,635
doesn't make sense to me.

1769
01:18:06,636 --> 01:18:09,839
When people's basic rights are
being violated,

1770
01:18:09,840 --> 01:18:11,874
you can call it "development,"

1771
01:18:11,875 --> 01:18:13,642
but the society is rotten underneath.

1772
01:18:13,643 --> 01:18:15,945
If the foundation is rotten,
can we really say it's okay...

1773
01:18:15,946 --> 01:18:17,947
just because it looks good on the surface?

1774
01:18:18,449 --> 01:18:21,117
"Even if I starve, I like freedom."

1775
01:18:21,118 --> 01:18:23,052
I can't agree with that idea.

1776
01:18:23,053 --> 01:18:26,289
Without a basic level
of material security,

1777
01:18:26,290 --> 01:18:29,658
human dignity is difficult to preserve.

1778
01:18:29,659 --> 01:18:32,628
When you're hungry and starving,

1779
01:18:33,029 --> 01:18:35,464
it's difficult to talk
about freedom and human rights.

1780
01:18:35,925 --> 01:18:36,858
We won!

1781
01:18:37,401 --> 01:18:38,334
We won!

1782
01:18:38,335 --> 01:18:39,302
(Today's MVP)

1783
01:18:39,303 --> 01:18:41,170
Today, I mainly focused...

1784
01:18:41,471 --> 01:18:43,772
on what these people were looking for.

1785
01:18:43,773 --> 01:18:47,244
They don't really care much
about the outcome of the debate.

1786
01:18:47,245 --> 01:18:51,514
They pay more attention to whether
the debaters listen to the other side.

1787
01:18:51,515 --> 01:18:55,818
I voted for totentanz.

1788
01:18:55,819 --> 01:18:57,486
Even though she was on the opposing side,

1789
01:18:58,515 --> 01:19:01,817
she summarized our side's arguments.

1790
01:19:01,992 --> 01:19:04,593
She also agreed with some of the points
we made...

1791
01:19:04,594 --> 01:19:06,696
and seemed really open-minded.

1792
01:19:07,230 --> 01:19:09,631
Even though I was on the other side,

1793
01:19:09,900 --> 01:19:11,299
I really appreciated that.

1794
01:19:11,300 --> 01:19:14,103
Totentanz is really thoughtful.

1795
01:19:14,437 --> 01:19:18,206
I felt like it was time
to give her an MVP...

1796
01:19:18,207 --> 01:19:19,875
because she works really hard.

1797
01:19:25,916 --> 01:19:30,319
Depending on whether I've received
my prize money,

1798
01:19:31,121 --> 01:19:32,254
that will determine...

1799
01:19:34,057 --> 01:19:35,824
whether to bet today.

1800
01:19:36,893 --> 01:19:41,396
Starting tonight, there will be
a belief check every night.

1801
01:19:42,332 --> 01:19:44,466
If you fail, you get a penalty,

1802
01:19:44,734 --> 01:19:48,236
which starts at 1,000 dollars today.

1803
01:19:48,545 --> 01:19:50,939
It increases by 1,000 dollars each day,

1804
01:19:50,940 --> 01:19:53,741
and on Day Six, the final day,

1805
01:19:53,742 --> 01:19:56,044
the penalty will be 5,000 dollars.

1806
01:19:56,413 --> 01:19:58,381
Also, if you still have...

1807
01:19:58,382 --> 01:20:02,618
your basic attack card unused by Day Six,

1808
01:20:03,019 --> 01:20:06,121
you lose another 5,000 dollars
as an opportunity cost.

1809
01:20:06,456 --> 01:20:11,060
If the penalty is
more than the money you have,

1810
01:20:11,294 --> 01:20:15,264
you only lose whatever money you have.

1811
01:20:15,698 --> 01:20:19,001
So, if you have no money,

1812
01:20:19,002 --> 01:20:23,506
you can use your attack card
without worrying about the penalty.

1813
01:20:23,940 --> 01:20:25,307
I have nothing now.

1814
01:20:25,308 --> 01:20:28,010
This is my only chance to attack...

1815
01:20:28,311 --> 01:20:29,878
without losing anything.

1816
01:20:29,879 --> 01:20:33,415
Even if I get it wrong,
I don't lose anything...

1817
01:20:33,416 --> 01:20:35,317
because my balance is zero.

1818
01:20:44,528 --> 01:20:45,595
My money came in today.

1819
01:20:45,862 --> 01:20:47,529
Gosh. It should've been tomorrow.

1820
01:20:48,431 --> 01:20:51,400
The prize money
should've been transferred tomorrow...

1821
01:20:52,569 --> 01:20:54,003
so I could bet today.

1822
01:20:54,670 --> 01:20:57,306
Yesterday, I lost the debate,
and someone else became the MVP,

1823
01:20:57,307 --> 01:20:59,174
so I didn't know the money was paid out
on the same day.

1824
01:20:59,175 --> 01:21:01,009
My biggest mistake today was...

1825
01:21:01,034 --> 01:21:03,035
participating in the debate.

1826
01:21:03,447 --> 01:21:06,014
My balance should've been zero!

1827
01:21:06,649 --> 01:21:10,052
The favorability vote will now begin.

1828
01:21:20,363 --> 01:21:24,466
People who seem least likely to get votes.

1829
01:21:24,467 --> 01:21:28,203
Three people: Nangja, Michael, and Hippo.

1830
01:21:28,204 --> 01:21:30,806
(Dark Knights favorability vote:
Nangja, Michael, and Hippo)

1831
01:21:30,807 --> 01:21:32,007
I tried it today...

1832
01:21:32,008 --> 01:21:33,345
and it was different from a verbal debate.

1833
01:21:33,346 --> 01:21:34,843
I'm a slow typer.

1834
01:21:35,679 --> 01:21:38,547
By the time I was about to respond,

1835
01:21:38,548 --> 01:21:40,082
they had already said...

1836
01:21:40,083 --> 01:21:43,685
what they wanted to say and moved on.

1837
01:21:44,354 --> 01:21:47,924
Just like in George Orwell's
"Animal Farm,"

1838
01:21:47,949 --> 01:21:50,583
you can already see
an elite class starting to emerge here.

1839
01:21:50,860 --> 01:21:53,662
At the same time, some people are
getting pushed to the side.

1840
01:21:54,364 --> 01:21:56,899
If we don't bring them along with us,

1841
01:21:56,900 --> 01:21:59,368
they could team up and turn against us.

1842
01:21:59,369 --> 01:22:01,003
There's something I know for sure.

1843
01:22:01,905 --> 01:22:03,739
I made a lot of inferences today.

1844
01:22:04,174 --> 01:22:05,474
I could tell who Polar Bear was.

1845
01:22:05,475 --> 01:22:06,742
I could easily identify two people.

1846
01:22:06,743 --> 01:22:08,814
You could even tell
from the way they talked.

1847
01:22:08,815 --> 01:22:11,980
- Superman and Polar Bear.
- Exactly.

1848
01:22:11,981 --> 01:22:13,649
It was obviously Polar Bear.

1849
01:22:13,650 --> 01:22:16,251
It was so obvious
that luminous1101 was Polar Bear.

1850
01:22:16,252 --> 01:22:19,688
She said exactly what I was thinking.

1851
01:22:19,689 --> 01:22:20,924
I could tell who they were.

1852
01:22:20,925 --> 01:22:21,957
Polar Bear.

1853
01:22:25,295 --> 01:22:27,696
I think there are more people
who have figured each other out.

1854
01:22:27,931 --> 01:22:30,132
I think so.
I'm sure there are a lot of them.

1855
01:22:30,133 --> 01:22:31,300
There must be a lot.

1856
01:22:32,836 --> 01:22:33,769
It was obvious...

1857
01:22:33,970 --> 01:22:34,970
that you were blackpink.

1858
01:22:36,206 --> 01:22:37,941
Me? No, I'm not.

1859
01:22:37,942 --> 01:22:39,041
- No?
- No.

1860
01:22:39,542 --> 01:22:41,209
You really have a certain image of me.

1861
01:22:41,511 --> 01:22:42,711
It sounded so much like you.

1862
01:22:43,347 --> 01:22:45,147
Even the way they laid out the points.

1863
01:22:47,784 --> 01:22:48,917
You're the most experienced at it.

1864
01:22:48,918 --> 01:22:50,052
- Spare me.
- What?

1865
01:22:50,053 --> 01:22:50,953
Spare me.

1866
01:22:52,789 --> 01:22:54,723
I basically went back and forth...

1867
01:22:54,724 --> 01:22:57,259
with Superman all day today.

1868
01:22:57,260 --> 01:23:00,195
That debater sounded just like him.

1869
01:23:00,196 --> 01:23:02,197
The way they talked,

1870
01:23:02,198 --> 01:23:05,934
the expressions they used,
and the way they developed their argument.

1871
01:23:06,102 --> 01:23:08,303
I really gave myself away. I'm in trouble.

1872
01:23:14,744 --> 01:23:17,312
(Sugar)

1873
01:23:17,681 --> 01:23:19,115
(Michael)

1874
01:23:20,050 --> 01:23:22,752
Did she keep her promise?

1875
01:23:24,621 --> 01:23:26,355
Shall we give each other a heart?

1876
01:23:26,356 --> 01:23:27,856
Yes! A heart exchange!

1877
01:23:27,857 --> 01:23:29,458
- Let's have each other's backs.
- Yes.

1878
01:23:29,459 --> 01:23:31,860
If we give each other a heart,
we secure two hearts at least.

1879
01:23:31,861 --> 01:23:33,428
I know!

1880
01:23:33,429 --> 01:23:34,697
A cute heart alliance.

1881
01:23:37,133 --> 01:23:38,367
(Hippo)

1882
01:23:40,303 --> 01:23:42,237
But Dark Knight gave her money.

1883
01:23:43,673 --> 01:23:44,773
Be honest. You're broke, right?

1884
01:23:44,940 --> 01:23:46,441
Me? Yes, I am.

1885
01:23:46,609 --> 01:23:48,076
Please give it to Hippo.

1886
01:23:48,101 --> 01:23:49,235
Really?

1887
01:23:49,813 --> 01:23:51,012
Thank you.

1888
01:23:51,314 --> 01:23:52,214
Pay me with a heart.

1889
01:23:54,718 --> 01:23:56,152
I don't think she chose me.

1890
01:23:59,323 --> 01:24:00,356
For now...

1891
01:24:00,357 --> 01:24:01,357
(Dark Knight)

1892
01:24:01,791 --> 01:24:04,626
(Genie's favorability vote:
Dark Knight, Michael, Sugar)

1893
01:24:05,196 --> 01:24:07,896
I voted for Michael,

1894
01:24:08,465 --> 01:24:12,768
Polar Bear, and Hippo.

1895
01:24:13,569 --> 01:24:17,706
(The heart alliance has fallen apart.)

1896
01:24:25,891 --> 01:24:28,291
Would you send me a heart
instead of attacking me?

1897
01:24:28,316 --> 01:24:29,618
- Let's send each other a heart.
- Send a heart?

1898
01:24:29,619 --> 01:24:31,186
- Let's give each other a heart.
- Really?

1899
01:24:31,187 --> 01:24:33,889
I'll keep my promise. I'm loyal.

1900
01:24:35,024 --> 01:24:39,694
I kept my word to the people
I made a deal with today.

1901
01:24:39,695 --> 01:24:43,231
Michael, Sugar, and Genie.

1902
01:24:44,567 --> 01:24:46,968
If you try a belief check...

1903
01:24:46,969 --> 01:24:49,738
and get it wrong, you lose 1,000 dollars.

1904
01:24:49,906 --> 01:24:52,673
And it says it'll go up every day.

1905
01:24:52,674 --> 01:24:55,611
So it'll be 2,000 dollars tomorrow,
then 3,000 dollars the day after.

1906
01:24:55,779 --> 01:24:57,780
Actually, if you think about it,

1907
01:24:57,781 --> 01:25:00,784
you have to do it at least once.

1908
01:25:00,785 --> 01:25:03,718
I did some math in my room and realized...

1909
01:25:03,719 --> 01:25:07,090
this is the most important issue
we need to discuss today.

1910
01:25:07,091 --> 01:25:07,957
"We need to discuss?"

1911
01:25:07,958 --> 01:25:10,025
So as many people as possible can survive.

1912
01:25:10,627 --> 01:25:12,661
As we get further into the game,

1913
01:25:12,662 --> 01:25:16,031
there'll be more pressure
to hurt each other.

1914
01:25:16,032 --> 01:25:17,633
I think we should talk about this...

1915
01:25:17,634 --> 01:25:22,437
and agree on a strategy
before that pressure starts building.

1916
01:25:22,540 --> 01:25:25,507
If you don't want to hurt someone,

1917
01:25:26,109 --> 01:25:28,410
you can just use your attack card first.

1918
01:25:29,479 --> 01:25:31,781
The spy can only take the prize money...

1919
01:25:31,782 --> 01:25:35,084
from the residents
who fail the final game.

1920
01:25:35,485 --> 01:25:39,721
The more residents that make it
to the final game,

1921
01:25:39,722 --> 01:25:43,158
the more money the spy can take home.

1922
01:25:43,660 --> 01:25:45,193
I want everyone...

1923
01:25:45,194 --> 01:25:46,361
to save a lot and survive...

1924
01:25:46,362 --> 01:25:48,831
so I can fatten my own prize money.

1925
01:25:48,832 --> 01:25:50,598
I don't want to send anyone home.

1926
01:25:50,800 --> 01:25:52,301
I want to make my cut big.

1927
01:25:54,370 --> 01:25:57,172
The favorability vote has ended.

1928
01:25:57,173 --> 01:26:02,911
(Favorability Vote Status)

1929
01:26:03,713 --> 01:26:06,615
All official activities
for today are over.

1930
01:26:06,616 --> 01:26:09,351
The Community Center is now closed.

1931
01:26:09,953 --> 01:26:12,221
- It'll only get harder.
- Really?

1932
01:26:12,456 --> 01:26:15,357
So the belief check is
starting today, right?

1933
01:26:15,668 --> 01:26:17,234
It said it was open.

1934
01:26:17,259 --> 01:26:20,562
As long as we stick to the rules so far,

1935
01:26:20,563 --> 01:26:23,064
I think it's possible for everyone...

1936
01:26:23,065 --> 01:26:26,134
to make it to the end,

1937
01:26:26,135 --> 01:26:29,171
unless another rule comes up...

1938
01:26:29,172 --> 01:26:31,306
that says someone has to be eliminated.

1939
01:26:31,841 --> 01:26:32,976
I agree.

1940
01:26:32,977 --> 01:26:34,613
If you think about the Mafia Game,

1941
01:26:34,614 --> 01:26:37,479
we lose if the mafia survives at the end.

1942
01:26:37,480 --> 01:26:39,848
But we just have a spy.

1943
01:26:40,316 --> 01:26:43,452
We could just ignore the spy...

1944
01:26:43,686 --> 01:26:45,854
- And go to the end.
- and just go to the end.

1945
01:26:46,623 --> 01:26:48,023
That's also possible.

1946
01:26:48,024 --> 01:26:50,960
- The spy is still a resident.
- He could be reformed.

1947
01:26:50,961 --> 01:26:52,494
"Reformed."

1948
01:26:52,996 --> 01:26:55,064
What if that's the most ideal situation?

1949
01:26:55,065 --> 01:26:56,865
What if that's our ultimate goal?

1950
01:26:58,310 --> 01:27:02,113
People are starting to think
that most of us could make it to the end.

1951
01:27:02,138 --> 01:27:04,572
It could be possible.
If we trust each other,

1952
01:27:04,573 --> 01:27:07,075
keep our greed under control,
and stick together,

1953
01:27:07,076 --> 01:27:09,277
we could all walk away...

1954
01:27:09,278 --> 01:27:12,747
with some money and be happy
even if we don't make a fortune.

1955
01:27:12,748 --> 01:27:15,817
Some people said
that we could try to play the game...

1956
01:27:15,818 --> 01:27:18,286
without eliminating anyone.

1957
01:27:18,287 --> 01:27:20,722
Some people were arguing that.

1958
01:27:21,024 --> 01:27:22,992
I could tell where they stand.

1959
01:27:22,993 --> 01:27:26,194
I agree with the idea to some extent,
that's not very realistic.

1960
01:27:26,195 --> 01:27:27,896
Some said
we don't necessarily have to find...

1961
01:27:27,897 --> 01:27:30,298
and eliminate the spy.

1962
01:27:30,299 --> 01:27:31,766
I hope...

1963
01:27:31,767 --> 01:27:34,336
they keep thinking
we're all good people and peaceful...

1964
01:27:34,337 --> 01:27:37,706
and they don't have to get rid
of Benjamin.

1965
01:27:38,041 --> 01:27:39,708
I'll work hard for that.

1966
01:27:39,709 --> 01:27:42,177
If we can find a way
for all of us to survive,

1967
01:27:42,879 --> 01:27:45,414
I think it's worth trying.

1968
01:27:45,415 --> 01:27:47,549
That'd be the best outcome.

1969
01:27:53,157 --> 01:27:54,656
I thought...

1970
01:27:54,657 --> 01:27:57,526
To be honest,
Benjamin is the easiest target.

1971
01:27:57,727 --> 01:28:00,595
His score is four, and he speaks English.

1972
01:28:00,863 --> 01:28:02,330
I talked to him many times.

1973
01:28:02,331 --> 01:28:05,868
- Go Ae Shin also got a four.
- Right. Only those two.

1974
01:28:06,336 --> 01:28:08,404
We could eliminate them together.

1975
01:28:08,405 --> 01:28:09,237
Right.

1976
01:28:09,238 --> 01:28:11,407
Soon, we're all going to fight to survive.

1977
01:28:11,408 --> 01:28:15,210
This community is about to turn
into a belief-testing zone.

1978
01:28:15,345 --> 01:28:16,745
Tomorrow is important.

1979
01:28:18,582 --> 01:28:21,383
Today's leader is...

1980
01:28:21,384 --> 01:28:24,886
Benjamin.

1981
01:28:26,624 --> 01:28:28,657
You made a big mistake.
